[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 5, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical AI figure with panels labeled (a), (b), and (c).\n\n(a) Conceptual workflow diagram for a multimodal generative foundation model centered on H&E tissue images. It shows input/output modalities across biological scales: RNA bulk/spatial transcriptomics, proteomics outputs including IHC and mIF, cellular-scale cells, tissue H&E, and clinical text such as notes and pathology reports. Arrows distinguish “generated from H&E” and “to generate H&E.” A “Multimodal Diffusion Model (SiT)” connects to a “Generative Foundation Model.” A horizontal scale axis runs from nm molecular, to um cellular, to mm-cm tissue, to patient. The tissue H&E image is marked as a bridging modality, with a note about fresh frozen to FFPE.\n\n(b) Three horizontal bar charts summarizing dataset scale by tissue/site category. Left chart: “H&E images: 100 M,” with x-axis number of images in millions. Middle chart: “Text-image pairs: 1.6 M,” with x-axis number of text-image pairs in thousands. Right chart: “RNA-image pairs: 10.8 M,” with x-axis number of RNA-image pairs in thousands. Tissue labels include lung, breast, prostate, kidney, colon, brain, ovary, skin, rectum, heart, liver, uterus, stomach, skeletal muscle, adipose tissue, thyroid, pancreas, esophagus, testis, spleen, blood vessel, bone marrow, bladder, lymph node, oral cavity, salivary gland, adrenal gland, biliary tract, cervix, thymus, nerve, peritoneum, and eye, depending on chart.\n\n(c) Model architecture diagram labeled MUPAD. It depicts image, text, and RNA multimodal guidance conditioning a noisy latent H&E patch into a clean image through multimodal DiT blocks. A pretrained encoder and alignment CNN feed into the model. The right side zooms into an “Inside a DiT Block” schematic with input features, timestep t, self-attention, MLP, residual connections, and a decoupled cross-modal attention module containing image cross-attention, text cross-attention, and RNA cross-attention, combined with modality IDs."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 7, "caption": "Figure 2: Image generation conditioned with image or text prompts. a , Image-to-image generation. Representative examples and quantitative benchmarks demonstrate that MUPAD preserves authentic biological structures with greater fidelity than competing baselines, achieving superior Image-Image similarity and FID. b , Text-to-image generation. Visual examples illustrate that MUPAD accurately reconstructs fine-grained histological features from text prompts. MUPAD achieves superior performance across Image-Image similarity, Text-Image alignment, and FID. All metrics are presented as median values with 95% bootstrap confidence intervals. ↑ higher is better; ↓ lower is better.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure comparing histopathology image-generation models.\n\nVisible content:\n- Top section shows reference histology images on the left and generated image grids labeled `MUPAD`, `PixCell`, `FLUX.2`, and `SD 3.5`.\n- The biological source objects are H&E-like tissue microscopy patches, including glandular/epithelial structures, dense cellular tumor-like tissue, stromal regions, and nuclei-rich pathology images.\n- Each model column contains multiple generated patches intended to resemble the reference histology morphology.\n- `SD 3.5` outputs appear mostly blurred or non-histological purple texture patches in the top comparison.\n\nQuantitative panels:\n- Bar chart labeled `Image similarity`, with upward arrow `better`; `MUPAD` is highest, followed by `PixCell`, `FLUX.2`, and `SD 3.5`.\n- Bar chart labeled `FID`, with downward arrow `better`; `MUPAD` is lowest/best, followed by `PixCell`, `FLUX.2`, and `SD 3.5` worst.\n\nLower section labeled `(b) text-to-image generation`:\n- Columns are labeled `Prompts`, `Real images`, `MUPAD`, `PathLDM`, `SD v3.5`, and `FLUX.2`.\n- Prompt boxes contain pathology-style descriptions with highlighted terms such as glioma cells, round nuclei, eosinophilic cytoplasm, ependymoma, cuboidal cells, and eosinophilic cytoplasm.\n- Rows compare real histology images against model-generated outputs from text prompts.\n\nLower quantitative panels:\n- `FID` bar chart with downward arrow `better`; `MUPAD` performs best, while `SD 3.5` is worst.\n- `Image-Image sim.` bar chart with upward arrow `better`; `MUPAD` is highest.\n- `Text-Image sim.` bar chart with upward arrow `better`; `MUPAD` is highest and `SD 3.5` is near zero.\n\nMain finding shown:\n- `MUPAD` produces histology images most similar to references and text prompts across the displayed metrics, outperforming `PixCell`, `PathLDM`, `FLUX.2`, and Stable Diffusion variants in these comparisons."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 9, "caption": "Figure 3: Training data augmentation using MUPAD. a , Few-shot classification augmented with MUPAD via image-to-image generation. Augmenting with MUPAD-synthesised morphological variants consistently improves classification accuracy across both 5-shot and 10-shot settings on five evaluated datasets, demonstrating robust generalisation under data-scarce conditions. b , Pathology text-image retrieval augmented with MUPAD-generated image-text pairs. Fine-tuning a vanilla CLIP model on these synthetic pairs substantially improves both text-to-image (T2I) and image-to-text (I2T) retrieval at R@10 and R@50.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel bar-chart figure comparing baseline/pretrained versus augmented models.\n\nPanel (a), labeled “few-shot image classification,” contains two grouped bar charts:\n- “5-shot classification”\n- “10-shot classification”\nY-axis: Accuracy.\nX-axis datasets: SkinCancer, PanNuke, UniToPatho, LC25000, SICAPv2.\nLegend: Baseline in teal, Augmented in magenta.\nError bars and significance brackets are shown with labels such as P<0.05, P<0.01, P<0.1, and P<0.001.\nVisible finding: augmented performance is generally higher than baseline across datasets, especially for PanNuke and SICAPv2; LC25000 is high for both.\n\nPanel (b), labeled “Text-image retrieval,” contains six grouped bar charts arranged by retrieval direction and dataset:\n- Top row: I2T Recall for Book, Twitter, WebPathology\n- Bottom row: T2I Recall for the same three datasets\nX-axis metrics: R@10 and R@50.\nY-axis: Recall.\nLegend: CLIP (Pretrained) in teal, CLIP (Augmented) in magenta.\nSignificance brackets mostly show P<0.001.\nVisible finding: CLIP augmented substantially improves recall over pretrained CLIP for both image-to-text and text-to-image retrieval across Book, Twitter, and WebPathology datasets.\n\nNo biological objects are directly shown as images; the figure reports quantitative results for biomedical/pathology-related datasets. No visible model architecture or interface diagrams are present."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 11, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure comparing real and synthetic histology-derived cell distributions across cancer types and cell classes.\n\nPanel (a): Bar chart of FID scores for five cancer datasets: Bladder, DLBCL, GBM, Mesothelioma, and Ovarian. Two methods are compared: MUPAD in blue and GeneFlow in red. The y-axis is FID, with a downward arrow labeled “better,” indicating lower FID is preferred. MUPAD has lower FID than GeneFlow for all shown cancer types.\n\nPanel (b): Violin plots comparing Real vs Synthetic cell-type percentages. The y-axis is Percentage. Cell classes include Cancer cell, Fibroblasts, Muscle Cell, Lymphocytes, Endothelial Cell, Red blood cell, Macrophages, Apoptotic Body, Minor Stromal Cell, Neutrophils, Plasmocytes, Eosinophils, Epithelial, and Mitotic Figures. Red W-distance annotations appear below each class.\n\nPanel (c): Bar charts of W distance by cell type, comparing MUPAD and GeneFlow. Two stacked bar-chart groups cover major and minor cell classes. The y-axis is W distance on a log scale, with a downward “better” arrow. MUPAD generally shows smaller W distances than GeneFlow.\n\nPanel (d): Grid of histopathology image patches labeled Real and Synthetic for each cancer source: Bladder, DLBCL, GBM, Mesothelioma, and Ovarian. Rows are marked as same transcriptomics. Overlaid colored segmentation contours identify biological objects/cell categories. Legend includes Cancer cell, Lymphocytes, Fibroblasts, Endothelial Cell, Muscle Cell, Minor Stromal Cell, Apoptotic Body, Mitotic Figures, Epithelial, Macrophages, Neutrophils, Eosinophils, Plasmocytes, Red blood cell, and Unclassifiable.\n\nBiological source objects: cell nuclei or cellular objects in histology tissue images from multiple cancer types, with class-specific segmentation outlines.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces: comparison of synthetic histology images generated from transcriptomic conditions against real histology images; quantitative model comparison between MUPAD and GeneFlow using FID and Wasserstein distance.\n\nVisible finding: MUPAD produces lower FID and generally lower cell-distribution W distances than GeneFlow, while synthetic image patches visually preserve cell-type segmentation patterns corresponding to real samples under matched transcriptomic conditions."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 14, "caption": "Figure 5: Virtual H&E-to-IHC translation and clinical validation. a, Visual examples of multi-stain virtual IHC generation. Compared to CycleGAN and CUT, MUPAD provides more accurate spatial stain rendering. b, FID and KID scores demonstrate that MUPAD achieves better distributional fidelity and perceptual quality. c, Clinical utility on the IHC4BC dataset. When using virtual IHC images to predict ground-truth clinical biomarkers (H-scores for ER/PR/Ki67; amplification status for HER2), MUPAD yields the highest AUC performance across all markers. Sub-0.5 AUC values for baseline methods indicate systematic inverse correlations with clinical labels, as observed the semantic misalignment in stain generation.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical figure comparing image-to-image translation methods for histopathology/IHC stain generation.\n\nPanel (a) shows example image tiles arranged in columns labeled `H&E (input)`, `IHC (true)`, `MUPAD`, `CycleGAN`, and `CUT`. Rows are grouped by biomarker/dataset labels on the left: `HER2Match`, `IHC4BC-ER`, `IHC4BC-PR`, `IHC4BC-Her2`, and `IHC4BC-Ki67`. The visible biological source objects are breast cancer histology tissue micrographs/cell fields with hematoxylin/eosin morphology and brown immunohistochemical staining patterns. The transformation shown is virtual IHC prediction from H&E input, with generated outputs from MUPAD, CycleGAN, and CUT compared against true IHC.\n\nPanel (b) contains bar charts for quantitative image-distribution metrics, with `FID ↓` and `KID ↓` columns. For each biomarker/dataset row, MUPAD generally has lower FID and KID than CycleGAN and CUT. Example visible values include HER2Match FID 103.92 for MUPAD, 117.74 CycleGAN, 189.16 CUT; and KID 0.1146, 0.1559, 0.2683 respectively.\n\nPanel (c) contains AUC bar charts for downstream biomarker classification tasks labeled `ER`, `PR`, `Ki67`, and `Her2`. MUPAD has the highest visible AUC in all four plots: ER 0.8872, PR 0.8842, Ki67 0.9772, Her2 0.9556. CycleGAN and CUT are lower for ER/PR/Her2, while for Ki67 CUT 0.9021 is higher than CycleGAN 0.8736 but below MUPAD.\n\nOverall finding shown: MUPAD better matches true IHC appearance and yields better quantitative FID/KID and classification AUC than CycleGAN and CUT in the displayed comparisons."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 17, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical/computational pathology figure with panels labeled **(a)**, **(b)**, and **(c)**.\n\nPanel **(a)** shows a grouped bar chart of **Patch-level PCC** across marker channels: E-cadherin, Pan-CK, CD20, Hoechst, CD45, CD45RO, SMA, PD-L1, CD31, CD4, CD3e, Ki67, CD68, CD163, and CD8a. Methods compared are **MUPAD** in blue, **GigaTIME** in orange, and **HistoPlexer** in gray. Dashed horizontal lines indicate method averages. MUPAD is generally higher than the other methods for many markers.\n\nPanel **(b)** shows a grouped bar chart of **Slide-Level PCC** for similar markers. The same three methods are compared, again with dashed average lines. Hoechst has the highest correlations for all methods. MUPAD appears strongest overall across many markers, with GigaTIME and HistoPlexer lower on average.\n\nPanel **(c)** shows qualitative image examples arranged by marker categories: **stroma**, **epithelial markers**, **immune markers**, and **functional markers**. Columns include **SMA**, **E-cadherin**, **Pan-CK**, **CD45**, **CD3e**, **CD68**, **Ki67**, and **PD-L1**. Rows show **H&E image**, **True mIF**, and predicted outputs from **MUPAD**, **GigaTIME**, and **HistoPlexer**. Each predicted row includes a displayed **PCC** value per marker. The biological source objects are histology tissue regions/cells from H&E and multiplex immunofluorescence marker images. The transformation shown is prediction or translation from H&E morphology into marker-specific mIF-like signal maps by computational models. MUPAD examples visually resemble the true mIF more closely for several markers, while some GigaTIME and HistoPlexer outputs appear weaker, noisier, or less spatially matched."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 20, "caption": "Figure 7: Ablation studies of MUPAD. (a) Comparison of the proposed decoupled cross-attention (DCA) against shared cross-attention across multimodal embeddings. DCA consistently improves performance across image-to-image, text-to-image, and RNA-to-image generation tasks , with relative FID reductions of 13.6%, 7.9%, and 12.4%, respectively. (b) FID and image similarity trajectories for image-to-image generation of SiT, REPA, and MUPAD. Lower FID and higher image similarity indicate better performance. (c) Visual comparison of image-to-image generation across training steps (20K-100K) for MUPAD, REPA 48 , and SiT 26 .", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure comparing image generation/alignment methods for biological microscopy or histology data.\n\nPanel (a): Two grouped bar charts.\n- Left chart: y-axis “Image Similarity”; arrow “better” points upward. Three categories on x-axis: Image-to-Image, Text-to-Image, RNA-to-Image. Blue bars are “Shared CA (baseline)” and red bars are “DCA (MUPAD)”. DCA/MUPAD is higher than baseline in all three categories.\n- Right chart: y-axis “FID”; arrow “better” points downward. Same three categories. DCA/MUPAD has lower FID than the Shared CA baseline in all categories.\n\nPanel (b): Two line plots over training steps.\n- Left chart: y-axis “Image Similarity”; x-axis “Training (k steps)” with points at 20, 40, 60, 80, 100. Methods shown: MUPAD blue circles, REPA red squares, SiT green triangles. MUPAD improves substantially with training and remains above REPA and SiT.\n- Right chart: y-axis “FID”; x-axis “Training (k steps)”. Lower is better. MUPAD decreases more strongly and reaches the lowest FID by 100k steps, while REPA and SiT remain higher and close to each other.\n\nPanel (c): Grid of generated or reconstructed histology-like image patches.\n- Left side contains a “Reference” microscopy image showing pink/purple stained tissue with glandular or epithelial structures and nuclei.\n- Right side compares rows labeled MUPAD, REPA, and SiT across columns labeled training steps: 20k, 40k, 60k, 80k, 100k.\n- MUPAD images appear increasingly closer to the reference-like histology texture over training, while REPA and SiT rows show paler, less detailed tissue-like patches with a prominent white lumen/empty region.\n\nVisible biological source objects: stained histopathology or microscopy tissue patches with cellular nuclei, epithelial/gland-like structures, and pink-purple H&E-like coloration.\n\nVisible transformations/model interfaces: Image-to-Image, Text-to-Image, and RNA-to-Image generation or translation tasks; comparison of Shared CA baseline, DCA/MUPAD, REPA, and SiT models; evaluation by Image Similarity and FID across training steps.\n\nMain finding shown: DCA/MUPAD outperforms the Shared CA baseline in similarity and FID, and MUPAD improves with more training steps, achieving better image similarity and lower FID than REPA and SiT."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 31, "caption": "Extended Data Figure 1: Fresh frozen to FFPE image translation results. Visual comparison of translation quality on Lung and Brain tissues. MUPAD successfully suppresses cryo-artifacts typical of fresh frozen sections while synthesizing realistic H&E staining characteristics. Baselines often suffer from saturation (AIFFPE) or residual noise (CUT, CycleGAN). Quantitative evaluation showing MUPAD achieves the lowest FID (lower is better).", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure showing histology-style image translation/comparison results and quantitative metrics.\n\nVisible structure:\n- Top: a grid of microscopy image patches arranged by method columns.\n- Column labels: `Fresh Frozen`, `MUPAD`, `AI-FFPE`, `CUT`, `CycleGAN`.\n- Row group labels on the left: `Lung` and `Brain`.\n- Each tissue group contains two rows of image patches.\n- The patches appear to show hematoxylin-and-eosin-like tissue morphology with different color/stain appearances across methods.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Lung tissue microscopy patches.\n- Brain tissue microscopy patches.\n- The first column is labeled `Fresh Frozen`, suggesting source/reference frozen tissue images.\n- Other columns show transformed or generated appearances from model/method outputs.\n\nTransformations / model interfaces:\n- `MUPAD`\n- `AI-FFPE`\n- `CUT`\n- `CycleGAN`\n\nThese appear to be image-to-image translation or stain/domain transformation methods, compared against fresh frozen histology images.\n\nQuantitative panels:\n- Bottom left bar chart titled `Lung`.\n- Bottom right bar chart titled `Brain`.\n- Both charts have y-axis label `FID ↓`, indicating lower Fréchet Inception Distance is better.\n- Lung chart compares `MUPAD`, `AI-FFPE`, `CUT`, and `CycleGAN`, with approximate values increasing from MUPAD to CycleGAN.\n- Brain chart compares the same methods, with `CycleGAN` visibly highest and `MUPAD` lowest.\n\nVisible finding:\n- In both Lung and Brain FID plots, `MUPAD` has the lowest FID among the shown methods.\n- `CycleGAN` has the highest FID, especially for Brain."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 32, "caption": "Extended Data Figure 2: Generating H&E images from spatial transcriptomic. Synthetic samples preserve spatially resolved cell-type distributions for DLBCL, GBM, mesothelioma, and ovarian. The Wasserstein distance (W) quantifies the distribution alignment of real and synthetic. Lower is better.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific bar chart comparing **MUPAD** (blue) and **GeneFlow** (red) across four cancer datasets or biological contexts:\n\n- **DLBCL**\n- **GBM**\n- **MESOTHELIOMA**\n- **OVARIAN**\n\nEach dataset panel is split into two horizontal bar-chart rows, with cell-type categories on the x-axis and **“W distance”** on the y-axis. The y-axis appears logarithmic, with tick marks around `10^-2`, `10^-1`, `10^0`, and `10^1`. A downward arrow appears beside the y-axis label, suggesting lower W distance is better.\n\nVisible biological source objects / cell-type labels include:\n\n- Cancer cell\n- Fibroblasts\n- Lymphocytes\n- Macrophages\n- Endothelial Cell\n- Epithelial\n- Red blood cell\n- Apoptotic Body\n- Plasmocytes\n- Neutrophils\n- Minor Stromal Cell\n- Mitotic Figures\n- Eosinophils\n- Muscle Cell\n\nThe figure compares model outputs or methods, labeled only as **MUPAD** and **GeneFlow**, using W distance as the metric. No architecture diagrams, image transformations, or explicit model interfaces are shown.\n\nVisible findings: performance varies by dataset and cell type. In several categories the blue and red bars are similar, while in others one method has noticeably lower W distance. Examples visible in the figure include GeneFlow lower than MUPAD for some MESOTHELIOMA cell types such as fibroblasts and lymphocytes, while MUPAD is lower for some OVARIAN categories such as apoptotic body."]}]

