pressed genes along with their expression values\", \"carrier_family\": \"text_native_token_stream\", \"carrier_subtype\": \"serialized_biological_context_or_ordered_profile\", \"insertion_or_fusion_verbatim\": \"Crossmodal alignment is achieved through a Q-Former module with 32 query tokens\", \"fusion_topology\": \"query_bottleneck\", \"text_role\": \"biological_payload\", \"input_status\": \"actual_model_input\", \"evidence_quote\": \"Spanning 24 human organs with 483,152 single-cell profiles\", \"section_heading\": \"B.2 Collection of downstream Dataset\", \"supporting_figure_or_table\": null, \"evidence_status\": \"explicit_text\", \"uncertainty\": \"Model-visible form and fusion fields are inferred from the shared cell encoder description, not stated per-dataset.\"}, \"grounding\": {\"grounding_valid\": true, \"pages\": [9, 14, 18, 22], \"doc_item_refs\": [\"#/tables/4\", \"#/texts/357\", \"#/texts/361\", \"#/texts/362\", \"#/texts/428\", \"#/texts/429\", \"#/texts/430\", \"#/texts/431\"], \"provenance_match\": \"verbatim\", \"picture_only_provenance\": false, \"taxonomy_consistent\": true}}, {\"candidate_ref\": \"dense::full_2026-07-06__rec_001381::0094\", \"route\": {\"id\": \"TaxonomyCodedRoute_f647716188ecd353\", \"route_label\": \"Text-guided Pseudo-Cell Generation\", \"model_name\": \"scMMGPT\", \"lifecycle_phase\": \"evaluation\", \"task_or_configuration_verbatim\": \"text-conditioned pseudo-cell generation\", \"source_object_verbatim\": \"immune tissue [60] dataset\", \"source_object_normalized\": \"immune tissue dataset\", \"source_modality_normalized\": \"single-cell transcriptomics\", \"transformation_chain_verbatim\": [], \"transformation_chain_normalized\": [], \"model_visible_form_verbatim\": \"text-conditioned pseudo-cell generation\", \"carrier_family\": \"text_native_token_stream\", \"carrier_subtype\": \"plain_language_prompt_or_question\", \"insertion_or_fusion_verbatim\": \"text-conditioned\", \"fusion_topology\": \"side_or_generative_conditioning\", \"text_role\": \"instruction_or_query\", \"input_status\": \"actual_model_input\", \"evidence_quote\": \"scMMGPT achieves state-of-the-art performance in textconditioned pseudo-cell generation\", \"section_heading\": \"D.1 Text-guided Pseudo-Cell Generation\", \"supporting_figure_or_table\": \"Table 10\", \"evidence_status\": \"text_plus_figure\", \"uncertainty\": \"The excerpt does not show the exact prompt format.\"}, \"grounding\": {\"grounding_valid\": true, \"pages\": [19, 20], \"doc_item_refs\": [\"#/texts/506\", \"#/texts/508\", \"#/texts/509\", \"#/tables/9\"], \"provenance_match\": \"verbatim\", \"picture_only_provenance\": false, \"taxonomy_consistent\": true}}]\n\nComplete canonical Docling Markdown:\n## Language-Enhanced Representation Learning for Single-Cell Transcriptomics\n\nYaorui Shi 1* , Jiaqi Yang 1* , Changhao Nai 2 , Sihang Li 1 , Junfeng Fang 3 , Xiang Wang 1 , Zhiyuan Liu 3 † , Yang Zhang 3 †\n\n1 University of Science and Technology of China 2 Harbin Institute of Technology 3 National University of Singapore yaoruishi@gmail.com , acharkq@gmail.com , zhang@nus.edu.sg\n\n- ∗ Equal contribution. † Corresponding author.\n\n### Abstract\n\nSingle-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) offers detailed insights into cellular heterogeneity. Recent advancements leverage single-cell large language models (scLLMs) for effective representation learning. These models focus exclusively on transcriptomic data, neglecting complementary biological knowledge from textual descriptions. To overcome this limitation, we propose scMMGPT, a novel multimodal framework designed for language-enhanced representation learning in single-cell transcriptomics. Unlike existing methods, scMMGPT employs robust cell representation extraction, preserving quantitative gene expression data, and introduces an innovative two-stage pre-training strategy combining discriminative precision with generative flexibility. Extensive experiments demonstrate that scMMGPT significantly outperforms unimodal and multimodal baselines across key downstream tasks, including cell annotation and clustering, and exhibits superior generalization in out-of-distribution scenarios. Our code is available at https://github.com/syr-cn/scMMGPT .\n\n## 1 Introduction\n\nSingle-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) profiles gene expression at the level of individual cells, providing a fine-grained view of cellular heterogeneity [1-3]. The complexity and high dimensionality of scRNA-seq data necessitate powerful computational approaches that can leverage massive datasets efficiently and accurately [4]. Inspired by the success of large language models (LLMs) in natural language processing [5-7], specialized single-cell large language models (scLLMs) have emerged, leveraging self-supervised pre-training on extensive expression datasets to produce robust cell representations for downstream tasks such as cell annotation and clustering [8-11]. However, existing scLLMs are mostly pre-trained solely on scRNA-seq data, inherently constraining the breadth of their cell representations.\n\nIn this work, we explore language-enhanced single-cell representation learning , aiming to integrate the fine-grained molecular signals from scRNA-seq with the high-level biological knowledge encoded in textual descriptions and metadata. As shown in Figure 1, textual descriptions encode contextual information-such as species, tissue origin, and cell type-that is not directly captured by expression profiles but is critical for downstream biological interpretation. Incorporating such textual knowledge offers a promising path toward more comprehensive and semantically meaningful cell representations.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a scientific schematic/figure panel showing gene expression values for selected gene IDs. It contains:\n\n- A small circular biological object illustration at left, resembling a patterned cell or tissue-like sphere.\n- A horizontal chart with label `Expr Values` on the y-axis area and `Gene IDs` along the x-axis.\n- Blue vertical bars for genes:\n  - `SEC22B` with value `3`\n  - `MT-CO2` with value `127`\n  - `RPL37` with value `18`\n  - `MT-CYB` with value `69`\n  - `MALAT1` with value `88`\n- A scale note at lower right: `[thousands of genes]`\n- No visible panel letter, legend, model interface, algorithmic transformation, or experimental workflow beyond the expression-value bar visualization.\n\nFinding shown: among the displayed genes, `MT-CO2` has the highest expression value, followed by `MALAT1` and `MT-CYB`; `SEC22B` is lowest.\n\nThe scRNA-seq Data of a cell\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nNON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image is a UI-style text snippet/card, not a scientific figure. It shows an orange document icon and a rounded gray callout containing text about a “classical monocyte” from the lung of *Homo sapiens* and a female donor. No plotted data, microscopy, panels, axes, experimental labels, biological objects, transformations, model interface, or scientific findings are visibly presented beyond the text description.\n\nThe Textual Description of a cell\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a bar chart panel labeled “(a)” comparing accuracy (%) across two cell groups: “All Cells” and “Test Cells.” The y-axis is “Accuracy (%)” with values roughly from 86 to 100. Two series are shown in the legend: “Expression Values” in blue and “Cell Sentence” in orange.\n\nVisible findings:\n- For “All Cells,” Expression Values accuracy is near 99%, higher than Cell Sentence at about 93–94%.\n- For “Test Cells,” Expression Values accuracy is about 96–97%, higher than Cell Sentence at about 93%.\n- Error bars are shown for all bars, indicating variability or uncertainty, but the exact statistic is not specified.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Cells are referenced as the evaluated biological objects, specifically “All Cells” and “Test Cells.”\n- No cell images, tissues, organisms, genes, or microscopy data are directly visible.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The chart compares two input or representation types: “Expression Values” and “Cell Sentence.”\n- No model architecture, workflow, or transformation pipeline is shown in this cropped panel.\n\nFigure 2: Cell type annotation results with different cell representation methods. (a) Cell type annotation accuracies on the full dataset and test set. Using cell sentences as cell representation leads to significant accuracy degradation. (b-d) UMAP visualization of classification results and the ground truth. Classification using cell sentences yields a lower accuracy score and exhibits poorer recognition capabilities in certain cell clusters.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a scientific visualization panel labeled “(c)” with the caption “Error cases with Cell Sentence.” It shows a 2D scatter/embedding plot of many small points, likely representing cell-level objects or samples. Points are colored by classification outcome: gray for “Correct” and red for “Incorrect,” according to the legend in the lower right.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Panel label: “(c)”\n- Title/annotation: “Error Rate: 6.42%” in red at the top right\n- Legend: gray = Correct, red = Incorrect\n- Main plot: irregular clustered point cloud/embedding with red incorrect cases distributed throughout several regions\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- The label “Cell Sentence” suggests the plotted objects are cell-derived representations or cell samples, but the image itself does not specify the organism, tissue, assay, or modality.\n\nTransformation/model interface:\n- The figure appears to visualize model prediction errors over a low-dimensional embedding or spatial projection of cell representations.\n- The model/interface named in the visible text is “Cell Sentence.”\n\nFinding shown:\n- The reported error rate is 6.42%.\n- Incorrect predictions are present across the embedding, with some local concentrations, while most points are marked correct.\n\nFigure 1: Comparison between the scRNA-seq results and the textual descriptions of a cell.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a small scientific plot labeled “Error cases with Expression Values” with panel marker “(b)”. It shows a 2D embedding/scatter plot, likely of biological observations such as cells or spots, rendered as many gray points.\n\nVisible elements:\n- A legend at lower right with gray points labeled “Correct” and red points labeled “Incorrect”.\n- Red text in the upper right: “Error Rate: 0.76%”.\n- Gray point clusters forming several separated manifold-like regions.\n- Sparse red points overlaid on the gray embedding, marking error cases.\n- No visible axes labels, tick marks, scale bars, or biological object names.\n- No visible model architecture or interface, only model/result classification correctness over an embedding.\n\nFinding shown:\n- The plotted error cases are rare and scattered across the embedding, with an overall reported error rate of 0.76%.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a scientific figure panel labeled “Ground Truth (d)”. It shows a 2D scatter/embedding plot with many colored points grouped into distinct clusters. A legend on the right lists biological cell-type labels:\n\n- B cells\n- CD4 T cells\n- CD8 T cells\n- CD14+ Monocytes\n- Dendritic Cells\n- Megakary. Monocytes\n- FCGR3A+ Monocytes\n- NK cells\n- Other\n\nVisible finding: the ground-truth annotation separates immune cell populations into colored clusters in a low-dimensional embedding. CD14+ monocytes form a large orange cluster, CD8 T cells appear as a blue upper cluster, NK cells as a red right-side cluster, and other labeled cell types form smaller neighboring clusters. No model interface, preprocessing transform, or method name is visible beyond the ground-truth panel label.\n\nTo harness the rich biological knowledge in text descriptions for single-cell analysis, recent studies have explored joint cell-text modeling [12-15]. However, we find that these efforts often overlook key aspects of language-enhanced cell representation learning:\n\n- Insufficient Unimodal Cell Representation Learning. Robust unimodal cell representation learning is the foundation before incorporating language enhancement. However, many existing works rely on general-purpose text LLMs or randomly initialized cell encoders, lacking the critical biological prior learned from large-scale scRNA-seq data for single-cell analysis [15, 16, 13]. Other methods represent cells using only the top 30-100 expressed genes [17, 18], or discard critical quantitative expression information and low-abundance genes [8, 19, 12]. They result in substantial information loss and reduced annotation accuracy ( cf. Figure 2).\n- Incomplete Cell-Text Alignment. Effectively leveraging textual knowledge requires comprehensive cross-modal alignment. Current cell-text models mainly focus on the development of text generation ability with generative objectives to support more flexible human interaction [17, 15, 19]. Nevertheless, real-world scientific discovery poses more demands on high-quality cell representations that can support more precise cell type annotation and clustering. To improve the performance on these crucial tasks, we also emphasize the importance of discrimination objectives, which help the model distinguish correct cell-type labels for more accurate cell annotation.\n\nTo address these gaps, we propose scMMGPT ( S ingleC ell M ulti M odal G enerative P re-trained T ransformer), a language-enhanced cell representation learning framework designed for single-cell transcriptomic analysis. Rather than exploring chat-based interfaces with text LLMs [15, 17, 19], scMMGPT emphasizes improved performance in essential single-cell analysis tasks with information from the text modality. The overall architecture of scMMGPT is shown in Figure 3. To ensure robust cell representation learning , scMMGPT preserves critical quantitative gene expression information in the tokenization processes. Furthermore, we construct comprehensive pre-training datasets comprising 27 million cells with scRNA-seq data from CellxGene [20] and textual descriptions from Wikipedia and OBO Foundry [21], guaranteeing rich biological prior.\n\nTo achieve comprehensive cell-text alignment , scMMGPT incorporates two separate projectors for the bidirectional information sharing between scLLM and text LLM, and a comprehensive two-stage pre-training strategy with both discriminative and generative objectives. The discriminative stage first aligns cell and text representations into a joint latent space and establishes cell annotation capability by training the model to distinguish the correct text description given scRNA-seq data. Concurrently, the generative stage promotes semantic alignment by reconstructing textual descriptions from cell embeddings and vice versa.\n\nTable 1: Comparison of unimodal scLLMs and cell-text multimodal LLMs.\n\n|                    |                      | Cell Representation Learning   | Cell Representation Learning   | Cell-Text Alignment Strategies   | Cell-Text Alignment Strategies   |\n|--------------------|----------------------|--------------------------------|--------------------------------|----------------------------------|----------------------------------|\n| Model              | w/ Textual Knowledge | Strong Bio. Insights           | No Info. Loss                  | Discriminative                   | Generative                       |\n| scGPT [9]          | ×                    | ✓                              | ✓                              | ×                                | ×                                |\n| scFoundation [11]  | ×                    | ✓                              | ✓                              | ×                                | ×                                |\n| scMulan [14]       | ×                    | ✓                              | ✓                              | ×                                | ✓                                |\n| GenePT [16]        | ✓                    | ×                              | ✓                              | ×                                | ×                                |\n| LangCell [12]      | ✓                    | ✓                              | ×                              | ✓                                | ×                                |\n| C2S [17]           | ✓                    | ×                              | ×                              | ×                                | ✓                                |\n| CellWhisperer [19] | ✓                    | ✓                              | ×                              | ✓                                | ✓                                |\n| InstructCell [15]  | ✓                    | ×                              | ✓                              | ×                                | ✓                                |\n| scMMGPT (Ours)     | ✓                    | ✓                              | ✓                              | ✓                                | ✓                                |\n\nWith these breakthroughs, scMMGPT significantly surpasses existing unimodal and multimodal methods, establishing a new standard for leveraging textual knowledge in single-cell transcriptomics analysis. Our method achieves steady improvement in cell type annotation with approximately 10% improved F1 scores across all benchmarks (§4.1.1) and much better accuracies under out-ofdistribution settings (§4.3). It generates cell representations with higher biological signals and less batch effect on both common cells and disease cells (§4.1.2). The cell description generated also contains more accurate cell type information (§4.1.3). Comprehensive ablation studies further validate the effectiveness of the key components (§4.2).\n\n## 2 Related Works\n\nSingle-Cell LLMs. Single-cell sequencing technologies provide diverse biological features that facilitate the interpretation of cellular structures and functions [22, 23]. Early efforts in scRNAseq analysis focused on statistical approaches such as Seurat [24] and Harmony [25]. Advances in scRNA-seq have also generated massive, high-precision transcriptomic datasets, driving the development of Single-Cell LLMs (scLLMs) [26]. This technique quantifies the mRNA molecule abundance, producing gene expression matrices that record expression values of individual genes across cells [27]. Previous works have developed transformer-based foundation models on scRNA-seq data, pre-training with masked learning objectives on millions of cells [28, 8, 10, 11]. Subsequent works improve the learning process by incorporating cell labels, such as batch information [9] and other cell metadata [14]. After fine-tuning, these LLMs have proven useful in practical downstream tasks such as cell-type annotation, cell clustering, and batch effect removal.\n\nCell-Text Modeling. Incorporating free-text biological descriptions has proven useful for improving cellular representation learning, with prior works demonstrating that such auxiliary textual supervision can enhance the quality of cell embeddings [16, 13, 29, 12]. Beyond representation learning, enabling bidirectional translation between cells and text facilitates tasks such as universal cell-type annotation [30] and the generation of pseudo-cells [31, 32]. Recent efforts aim to build multimodal LLMs for single-cell data that align cell and text modalities directly [15, 19]. After instruction tuning on cell annotation or pseudo-cell generation, these models enable interactive single-cell analyses for human users. Nevertheless, the most crucial single-cell analysis tasks that boost real-world scientific discoveries rely more on efficient cell representation extraction to support tasks like cell annotation and clustering, which is often neglected in previous multimodal approaches.\n\nScientific Multimodal LLMs. Multimodal LLMs show remarkable potential for integrating data from various modalities [33-35], inspiring research for scientific modalities. Existing works have constructed multimodal LLMs for small molecules [36-38] and proteins [39, 40] to tackle crossmodal scientific problems, such as description generation, molecular property prediction, and textconditioned de novo design [41-46]. Although single-cell analysis holds comparable scientific importance, the sparsity and high dimensionality nature of scRNA-seq data introduce a significant gap between transcriptomic and textual modalities, presenting challenges for joint modeling of them.\n\nFigure 3: Overview of scMMGPT. (1) Cross-modal Discriminative Objective: Given paired cell and text inputs, the model learns to identify the correct textual description of a cell by aligning the outputs of the scLLM and text LLM. (2, 3) Cross-modal Generative Objectives: scMMGPT strengthens multimodal alignment through a unified generative pre-training strategy, jointly optimizing cell-to-text and text-to-cell translation tasks to facilitate bidirectional knowledge transfer.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic workflow for multimodal cell information modeling using single-cell RNA-seq data and text.\n\nVisible components:\n\n- Left panel: “Multimodal Cell Information”\n  - Shows a human body/lung illustration with arrows labeled “scRNA-seq” and “Biological Databases.”\n  - Shows an “Experimental Evidence in scRNA-seq Matrices” block: a heatmap-like matrix with rows labeled Cell 1, Cell 2, …, Cell N and columns labeled Gene 1, Gene 2, …, Gene M.\n  - Shows “Domain Knowledge in Textual Descriptions”: an example text box describing a “classical monocyte,” sourced from human lung female donor samples.\n\n- Middle panel: “scRNA-seq Data”\n  - Gene expression values are transformed into “Gene Tokens.”\n  - Example gene-expression bar/sequence includes gene names such as SEC23B, MT-CO2, RPL37, MT-CYB and values like 127, 3, 18, 69.\n  - Text descriptions are transformed into a “Text Token Sequence,” with tokens such as “[BOS], This, cell, is, a, …, [EOS].”\n\n- Main model panel:\n  - A blue “scLLM” block processes gene tokens.\n  - An orange “Text LLM” block processes text tokens.\n  - Cross-modal projectors connect the two modalities:\n    - “Q-former Projector”\n    - “Cross-Attn Projection”\n  - Intermediate feature boxes are labeled “Cell Features” and “Text Features.”\n  - Arrows indicate cell-to-text and text-to-cell projection paths.\n  - A central label reads “Cross-modal Projectors.”\n\n- Right panel: output tasks/findings\n  - “1. Cell-Text Discrimination”: produces a “Relevance Score.”\n  - “2. Text-to-Cell Generation”: decodes text-derived information into a gene-expression/cell representation.\n  - “3. Cell-To-Text Generation”: decodes cell representation into a textual description, shown as the classical monocyte example.\n\nOverall, the figure depicts a multimodal architecture aligning scRNA-seq gene-token representations with textual biological descriptions using an scLLM, a text LLM, and cross-modal projection modules for discrimination and bidirectional generation tasks.\n\n## 3 Method\n\nTo build scMMGPT, we first construct a diverse cell-text dataset to support cross-modal training (§3.1). scMMGPT adopts a specialized scLLM that directly models original expression levels to enable robust cell representation learning and bypass information loss, alongside a pre-trained text LLM for generating descriptive annotations (§3.2). To facilitate comprehensive cell-text alignment, we introduce bidirectional projectors and a two-stage pre-training strategy combining discriminative with generative objectives (§3.3). After pre-training, scMMGPT can be applied to a range of downstream single-cell analysis tasks (§3.4). Figure 3 illustrates the overall architecture of scMMGPT.\n\n### 3.1 Large Scale and Multi-Source Cell-Text Data Collection\n\nLarge-Scale Single-Cell Transcriptomics Collection. For single-cell transcriptomics data, we collect 60 million single-cell profiles from the biggest single-cell transcriptomics database CellxGene [20]. The collected cellular data includes high-resolution scNRA-seq matrices with gene names and numeric expression levels and associated cell properties such as cell types, tissues, and disease statuses. To ensure data quality, we then conduct data filtering and deduplication (see Appendix B for more details). After these steps, we maintain scRNA-seq data of 27 million cells across various human tissues, summarized in Figure 4. The diverse cell atlas ensures generalization and prevents scMMGPT from degenerating into overfitting specific tissues.\n\nLarge-Scale Cell Description Collection. For textual information, we gather free-form cell identity explanation ( e.g., , definition of cell types and diseases) from two sources: (1) the OBO Foundry [21], which integrates professional reference for biomedical terms, and (2) Wikipedia, which contains comprehensible explanations of cell function. We construct textual descriptions of cells by merging the free-form cell explanations and the cell metadata from CellxGenes.\n\n### 3.2 Cell Representation Learning &amp; Language Generation with Pre-Trained Models\n\nTo leverage the strengths of both modalities, we utilize pre-trained models for unimodal cell representation learning and natural language generation. These models bring in domain-specific knowledge and robust in-domain feature extraction capabilities.\n\nscLLM for Cell Representation Learning. To obtain high-quality cell representations, we employ scGPT [9], a state-of-the-art single-cell language model pre-trained on large-scale scRNA-seq data. Its architecture is tailored to jointly model gene symbols and their quantitative expression levels, which avoids the loss of information that occurs with ranked gene lists.\n\nEach cell is represented as a vector derived from the raw count matrix X ∈ N N × M , where X ij denotes the expression level of gene j in cell i . Since absolute gene expression values can vary across measurement platforms, we apply row-wise normalization before feeding into the model:\n\nFigure 4: Summary of data used in the pre-training of scMMGPT. The dataset includes 27 million single-cell transcriptomic profiles from diverse human organs and tissues.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a biomedical infographic showing a human body diagram with organ and tissue callouts. It appears to summarize cell or tissue categories across anatomical locations, with counts in millions.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Central full-body anatomical illustration with highlighted organs, including brain, lungs, heart, liver, intestines, kidneys, reproductive region, and other tissue markers.\n- Green connector lines link organs/body regions to labeled text boxes around the figure.\n- Left-side labels:\n  - Peripheral Retina 1.20M\n  - Heart 0.68M\n  - Liver 0.44M\n  - Blood 4.18M\n  - Kidney 0.35M\n- Right-side labels:\n  - Brain 7.48M\n  - Lung 1.20M\n  - Breast 2.25M\n  - Others 6.46M\n  - Pan-Cancer 2.68M\n- Each label includes a small organ/cell icon and short biological text describing example cell types, such as amacrine cells, pericytes, hepatocytes, B cells, podocytes, neurons, pulmonary alveolus, myoepithelial cells, basal cells, and plasma cells.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Human organs and tissues: retina, heart, liver, blood, kidney, brain, lung, breast, miscellaneous tissues, and pan-cancer samples.\n- Cell types are mentioned in the explanatory snippets.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- No visible computational pipeline, model architecture, embeddings, plots, or transformation arrows are shown.\n- The image is a schematic/summary figure rather than a quantitative chart.\n\nFindings shown:\n- The figure visually associates organ/tissue categories with apparent dataset/sample sizes, e.g. Brain 7.48M, Others 6.46M, Blood 4.18M, Pan-Cancer 2.68M.\n- It provides brief descriptive definitions or examples for cell types in each tissue category.\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nTo mitigate data sparsity, we retain the top 2,048 most expressed genes per cell, which captures the majority of meaningful signals.\n\nText LLM for Text Generation. For the text LLM, we utilize LLaMA-2 7B [47], a decoder-only transformer that excels at text generation. Its extensive pre-training and generative architecture make it well-suited for biomedical text understanding and generation, such as describing cellular states. We tokenize the textual description of cells into a sequence of tokens t ( i ) = [ t ( i ) 1 , t ( i ) 2 , . . . , t ( i ) T ] using the tokenizer in text LLM.\n\n### 3.3 Comprehensive Cell-Text Alignment\n\nBuilding upon the pre-trained models, we achieve comprehensive cell-text alignment in scMMGPT via (1) bidirectional projectors and (2) a two-stage cross-modal pre-training strategy.\n\n#### 3.3.1 Bidirectional Projectors for Cell-Text Integration\n\nTo bridge the modality gap between cell and text LLMs, we introduce bidirectional cell-to-text and text-to-cell projectors that enable effective cross-modal alignment and information exchange.\n\n- The cell-to-text projector is implemented with a Query Transformer (Q-Former) [33] with 32 learnable queries, which maps high-dimensional cell embeddings from the scLLM into the token embedding space of the Text LLM. We initialize the Q-Former with weights from BiomedBERT [48], a BERT encoder trained on PubMed abstracts and biomedical literature [49].\n- The text-to-cell projector is realized using a stack of cross-attention layers [5] that project LLaMA-2 output embeddings into the hidden space of scGPT. The resulting representations are used as soft prompts [50], conditioning the scLLM for text-to-cell generation.\n\n#### 3.3.2 Cross-Modal Pre-training for Cell-Text Integration\n\nWe adopt a two-stage pre-training strategy to inject textual knowledge into the scLLM, align modalities, and enable bidirectional cell-text generation. Stage 1 aligns the representations and achieves coarse-grained knowledge injection through discriminative pre-training , while Stage 2 continues the knowledge integration and enables mutual cell-text translation via generative pre-training .\n\nFigure 5: The two-stage cross-modal pre-training scheme. (a) In cross-modal discriminative pretraining, the model achieves cell-text integration by distinguishing matched cell-text pairs from unrelated pairs through contrastive and matching objectives. (b) In cross-modal generative pretraining, the model continues knowledge integration via unified generative tasks, including cell-to-text and text-to-cell generation objectives.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic scientific figure with two labeled panels describing a cross-modal pretraining framework for single-cell and text models.\n\nPanel (a), “Stage 1: Cross-modal Discriminative Pre-training,” shows scRNA-seq input for “a B cell” represented as gene tokens/expression bars feeding into an scLLM. Text descriptions are divided into negative descriptions, such as mismatched cell types including megakaryocyte and natural killer cell, and a positive description identifying the cell as a B cell. The scLLM output and text descriptions connect through a Q-former projector with self-attention, cross-attention, and FFN blocks. The objective is labeled “Cell-Text Matching” and “Cell-Text Contrasting.”\n\nPanel (b), “Stage 2: Cross-modal Generative Pre-training,” has two workflows. The top “Cell-to-Text Generation” path sends scRNA/gene information through an scLLM, then a Q-former projector, then “Cell Features” into a Text LLM to generate a textual cell description. The bottom “Text-to-Cell Generation” path sends a B-cell text description through a Text LLM to produce embeddings, then through a cross-attention projector into an scLLM interface with gene tokens and expression levels.\n\nBiological source objects include scRNA-seq measurements from a B cell, gene tokens, expression-level bars, and cell-type descriptions. The figure illustrates transformations between single-cell gene-expression representations and natural-language descriptions using scLLM, Text LLM, Q-former, and cross-attention projection modules.\n\nStage 1: Cross-modal discriminative pre-training. We conduct cross-modal discriminative pretraining to create a shared latent space that captures semantic correspondences between scRNA-seq profiles and biomedical texts. Given a normalized single-cell expression vector ˜ x ( i ) ∈ R M , the scLLM produces a contextualized embedding h cell = scGPT ( ˜ x ( i ) ) . This is passed through a Qformer to yield the cell feature c = QFormer ( h cell ) . Similarly, for a textual description represented by the token sequence t ( i ) = { t 1 , . . . , t L } , we extract the text embedding via the BERT module within the Q-Former as h text = BERT ( t ( i ) ) .\n\nWe compute the alignment loss as a combination of the cell-text contrastive InfoNCE loss L InfoNCE and a cross-entropy cell-text matching loss L CE, thereby bringing paired representations closer while pushing unpaired ones apart:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nDuring this stage, parameters of both the cell encoder and Q-Former are updated.\n\nStage 2: Cross-modal generative pre-training. We further pre-train the model with cross-modal generative objectives to enhance the bidirectional knowledge injection:\n\n- Cell → Text Generation: Given a cell embedding processed by the cell encoder and projected via the cell-to-text module, we condition the decoder-only text LLM to autoregressively generate corresponding textual descriptions. The objective is defined as:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere t l denotes the l -th token of the generated text, and c represents the projected cell embedding.\n\n- Text → Cell Generation: In the reverse direction, we enable the generation of cell embeddings conditioned on textual input. We first generate intermediate embedding with the text LLM: c ′ = mlp ( Llama ( t ⩽ L )) . A lightweight text-to-cell projector then transforms this intermediate embedding into a soft prompt for the scLLM. The scLLM then predicts a pseudo-cell expression vector x ′ = scGPT ( c ′ ) ∈ R M . We optimize the gene prediction head of scGPT with a mean squared error (MSE) loss:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere ˜ x j denotes the ground-truth normalized expression.\n\nDuring this generative pre-training phase, we freeze the parameters of the cell-to-text projector and update the parameters of the text LLM, scLLM, and the text-to-cell projector by minimizing the combined loss L c2t + L t2c .\n\n### 3.4 Adapting scMMGPT to Downstream Tasks\n\nAfter two-stage pre-training, scMMGPT can be applied to various single-cell analysis tasks, either in a zero-shot manner or with additional fine-tuning.\n\nCell Type Annotation (§4.1.1). We leverage scMMGPT's ability to discern similarities between scRNA-seq profiles and cell-type text descriptions. In the zero-shot scenario, we directly use the stage-2 pre-trained scLLM and Q-former for annotation. Given a cell x ( i ) , we calculate its contrastive and matching losses against textual descriptions { t j } of all possible cell types, and select the class that minimizes the combined loss λ L InfoNCE +(1 -λ ) L CE as prediction. In the fine-tuning scenario, we continue training scMMGPT on the downstream dataset using the alignment loss L align, and employ the same inference approach as in the zero-shot case.\n\nBatch Effect Correction and Cell Clustering (§4.1.2). These tasks evaluate the quality of cell representations derived from the model. Specifically, we leverage the fully pre-trained scLLM ( i.e., scGPT) from both stages for zero-shot cell feature extraction. Formally, given a set of cells { x ( i ) } , we obtain their representations as c ( i ) = scGPT ( x ( i ) ) , which are then utilized for clustering.\n\nCell Description Generation (§4.1.3). For cell description generation tasks, we further fine-tune the text LLM with the cell-to-text translation loss L c2t, while freezing both the scLLM and the Q-former. During inference, we autoregressively generate descriptions conditioned on the cell embedding c until the end-of-sentence token.\n\n## 4 Experiments\n\nIn this section, we want to answer the following Research Questions (RQ):\n\nRQ1 : How effectively does scMMGPT enhance single-cell analysis tasks (cell type annotation, clustering, and text generation) by integrating biological priors from scRNA-seq and text?\n\nRQ2 : How do the discriminative and generative pre-training objectives synergistically contribute to the alignment and knowledge transfer between single-cell and text modalities?\n\nRQ3 : Does scMMGPT achieve robust generalization in challenging out-of-distribution scenarios?\n\nExperiment Setup The pre-training of scMMGPT consists of two stages: Stage 1 for representation alignment spans 5 epochs, while Stage 2 cross-modal generation runs for 1 epoch. Unless otherwise indicated, we apply LoRA [51] adapters for parameter-efficient fine-tuning of text LLM, whereas the cell encoder and projection modules are fully trained. Further details are provided in Appendix C.\n\n### 4.1 Effectiveness on Single-Cell Analysis Tasks (RQ1)\n\n#### 4.1.1 Cell Type Annotation\n\nThe cell type annotation task evaluates a model's ability to accurately classify cells based on their scRNA-seq profiles. We compare scMMGPT against several baselines: scBERT [10], scGPT [9], Geneformer [8], LangCell [12], and scELMo [13]. Accuracy and macro F1 score (F1) are used as evaluation metrics. We conduct experiments on four datasets: Myeloid [52], hPancreas [53], Multiple Sclerosis [54], and PBMC-3K [55]. All the models are fine-tuned on the downstream training dataset before the evaluation.\n\nThe experimental results are summarized in Table 2. Asterisk (*) denotes results borrowed from previous studies [9, 13]. scMMGPT consistently outperforms existing methods across all evaluated datasets, achieving steady improvements in accuracy and up to about 10% increase in F1 scores (Multiple Sclerosis and PBMC-3K). These results validate scMMGPT's superior classification performance and its strong adaptability across different biological contexts and tissue distributions.\n\nTable 2: Results of cell type annotation (%) with fine-tuning. Asterisk (*) denotes results borrowed from previous studies [9, 13]. Bold denotes best results.\n\n| Method         | Myeloid      | Myeloid      | hPancreas    | hPancreas   | Multiple Sclerosis   | Multiple Sclerosis   | PBMC-3K      | PBMC-3K      |\n|----------------|--------------|--------------|--------------|-------------|----------------------|----------------------|--------------|--------------|\n|                | Accuracy (%) | F1 (%)       | Accuracy (%) | F1 (%)      | Accuracy (%)         | F1 (%)               | Accuracy (%) | F1 (%)       |\n| scBERT [10]*   | 52.5         | 29.8         | 96.4         | 68.5        | 78.5                 | 59.9                 | 23.5         | 13.1         |\n| scGPT [9]*     | 64.2         | 34.6         | 96.8         | 71.8        | 85.6                 | 70.3                 | 93.3         | 80.7         |\n| Geneformer [8] | 59.3         | 35.6         | 96.6         | 77.3        | 71.3                 | 74.4                 | 86.4         | 65.0         |\n| LangCell [12]  | 58.9         | 35.7         | 96.3         | 70.8        | 72.9                 | 71.2                 | 90.6         | 81.2         |\n| scELMo [13]*   | -            | -            | 96.8         | 68.0        | -                    | -                    | 90.3         | 83.5         |\n| scMMGPT        | 69.0 +4 . 8  | 67.6 +31 . 9 | 98.2 +1 . 4  | 81.1 +3 . 8 | 87.4 +1 . 8          | 84.5 +10 . 1         | 94.8 +4 . 2  | 93.5 +10 . 0 |\n\nTable 3: Results of cell clustering on PBMC-10K [56] and COVID-19 [57] datasets.\n\n| Dataset       | Model          | Avg bio ( ↑ )   | NMI cell ( ↑ )   | ARI cell ( ↑ )   | ASW cell ( ↑ )   | Avg batch ( ↑ )   | Avg batch ( ↑ )   | ASW batch ( ↑   | ASW batch ( ↑   | 0.993 0.781 0.720 0.844 0.874 0.908   | 0.993 0.781 0.720 0.844 0.874 0.908   |\n|---------------|----------------|-----------------|------------------|------------------|------------------|-------------------|-------------------|-----------------|-----------------|---------------------------------------|---------------------------------------|\n|               | Seurat         | 0.724           | 0.808            | 0.722            | 0.641            | 0.940             |                   | 0.960           |                 | 0.920                                 |                                       |\n|               | Harmony [25]   | 0.784           | 0.860            | 0.902            | 0.591            | 0.940             |                   | 0.975           |                 |                                       | 0.906                                 |\n|               | scVI [32]      | 0.753           | 0.819            | 0.847            | 0.592            | 0.947             |                   | 0.967           |                 |                                       | 0.928                                 |\n| PBMC-10K [56] | scGPT [9]      | 0.821           | 0.850            | 0.873            | 0.740            | 0.923             |                   | 0.950           |                 |                                       | 0.895                                 |\n|               | Geneformer [8] | 0.793           | 0.825            | 0.846            | 0.709            | -                 |                   | 0.928           |                 |                                       | -                                     |\n|               | LangCell [12]  | 0.808           | 0.845 +0         | 0.854            | 0.724            | -                 |                   | 0.979           |                 | -                                     |                                       |\n|               | scMMGPT        | 0.854 +0 .      | 0.885 .          | 0.928 +0         | 0.748 +0 . 008   | 0.988 +0          | . 041             | 0.983 +0        | . 004           |                                       | +0 . 065                              |\n|               | Seurat [24]    | 0.413           | 0.513            | 0.289            | 0.437            | 0.790             |                   | 0.799           |                 |                                       |                                       |\n|               | Harmony [25]   | 0.327           | 0.482            | 0.185            | 0.313            | 0.680             |                   | 0.642           |                 |                                       |                                       |\n| COVID-19 [57] | scVI [32]      | 0.502           | 0.638            | 0.408            | 0.461            | 0.838             |                   | 0.833           |                 |                                       |                                       |\n|               | scGPT [9]      | 0.504           | 0.659            | 0.400            | 0.452            | 0.850             |                   | 0.826           |                 |                                       |                                       |\n|               | scMMGPT        | 0.545 +0 .      | 0.668 +0 .       | 0.454 +0         | 0.512 +0 . 051   | 0.892             | +0 . 042          | 0.875           | +0 . 042        |                                       | +0 . 034                              |\n\n#### 4.1.2 Cell Clustering\n\nCell clustering plays a fundamental role in novel cell type discovery, and helps eliminate the batch effects in scRNA-seq data, which are introduced by different wet-lab experiment batches. We evaluate scMMGPT on cell clustering tasks using the PBMC-10K [56] and COVID-19 [57] datasets, comparing it against several scLLMs and softwares: Seurat [24], Harmony [25], scVI [32], scGPT [9], Geneformer [8], and LangCell [12]. We use standard biological conservation (NMI cell , ARI cell , and ASW cell ) and batch correction (ASW batch and Graph Conn ) metrics [58] to assess performance, which can be summarized into Avgbio and Avgbatch scores. The detailed explanations of these metrics are in Appendix C.3.\n\nAs shown in Table 3, scMMGPT consistently outperforms all baselines across both biological conservation and batch correction metrics. On both datasets, scMMGPT achieves an Avgbio improvement of at least 0 . 03 and an Avgbatch improvement of at least 0 . 04 . These results highlight its strong ability to preserve biological structure while effectively correcting batch effects.\n\n#### 4.1.3 Cell Description Generation\n\nThe cell description generation task assesses how well a model can produce accurate textual descriptions from scRNA-seq data. We compare against GPT-2 [59] and C2S [17] on the immune tissue [60] dataset. We use Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD) and Earth Mover's Distance (EMD) to measure semantic similarity based on text embeddings [61], alongside BLEU [62], ROUGE [63], and METEOR [64] to measure rule-based text similarity. We also measure the cell annotation accuracy and F1 scores based on the cell type text extracted from the cell descriptions.\n\nAs shown in Table 4, scMMGPT substantially outperforms prior baselines, achieving higher text similarity and semantic alignment with ground-truth descriptions. It improves classification metrics by nearly 30%, rule-based text similarities by about 20%, and reduces MMD and EMD by nearly 50%. These results indicate the strong biological relevance in the descriptions generated by scMMGPT.\n\n### 4.2 Ablation Studies (RQ2)\n\nWe conduct systematic ablation studies to evaluate the effectiveness of each key component.\n\nImpact of training stages. We first assess the effectiveness of our multi-stage training process. We compare five different pre-training variations: (1) using the full pre-training pipeline, (2) using a randomly initialized scLLM instead of a pre-trained one, (3) removing L InfoNCE or L CE loss in stage 1 pre-training, (4) removing stage 2 pre-training, and (5) removing both stage 1&amp;2 pre-training. As shown in Table 5, the full training pipeline yields the best results, with clear improvements in both accuracy and F1-score.\n\nTable 4: Results of cell description generation on the immune tissue [60] dataset.\n\n| Model            | Accuracy ( ↑ )   | F1 ( ↑ )        | BLEU-2 ( ↑ )    | ROUGE-2 ( ↑ ) METEOR ( ↑ )      | MMD( ↓ )        | EMD ( ↓ )    |\n|------------------|------------------|-----------------|-----------------|---------------------------------|-----------------|--------------|\n| GPT-2 Small [59] | 21.96%           | 12.58%          | 36.82%          | 26.49% 38.61%                   | 0.189           | 0.020        |\n| GPT-2 Large [59] | 33.93%           | 15.99%          | 41.31%          | 35.18% 44.02%                   | 0.127           | 0.020        |\n| C2S Small [17]   | 35.05%           | 25.67%          | 50.07%          | 47.53% 55.38%                   | 0.043           | 0.016        |\n| C2S Large [17]   | 59.24%           | 54.97%          | 73.38%          | 68.54% 74.32%                   | 0.020           | 0.009        |\n| scMMGPT          | 87.56% +28 . 32  | 86.93% +31 . 95 | 91.45% +18 . 07 | 92.55% +24 . 01 90.72% +16 . 40 | 0.010 - 49 . 8% | 0.005 - 46 . |\n\nTable 5: Ablation studies of cell type annotation on various datasets.\n\n| Method                              | Myeloid                             | Myeloid                             | hPancreas                           | hPancreas                           | Multiple Sclerosis                  | Multiple Sclerosis                  | PBMC-3K                             | PBMC-3K                             |\n|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n|                                     | Accuracy                            | F1                                  | Accuracy                            | F1                                  | Accuracy                            | F1                                  | Accuracy                            | F1                                  |\n| scMMGPT                             | 68.96                               | 67.57                               | 98.22                               | 81.06                               | 87.38                               | 84.49                               | 94.83                               | 93.51                               |\n| Ablation over Training Stages       |                                     |                                     |                                     |                                     |                                     |                                     |                                     |                                     |\n| No Pre-train Stage 2                | 67.44                               | 66.78                               | 97.63                               | 80.56                               | 87.79                               | 83.40                               | 94.72                               | 93.36                               |\n| No Pre-train Stage 1&2              | 67.56                               | 63.22                               | 77.13                               | 44.03                               | 84.98                               | 76.45                               | 94.72                               | 91.41                               |\n| Stage 1 w/o L InfoNCE               | 64.21                               | 64.43                               | 93.62                               | 68.94                               | 86.41                               | 81.22                               | 94.50                               | 93.17                               |\n| Stage 1 w/o L CE                    | 68.26                               | 63.06                               | 96.70                               | 74.52                               | 86.82                               | 82.77                               | 94.61                               | 92.04                               |\n| scLLM from scratch                  | 66.13                               | 64.11                               | 97.18                               | 81.40                               | 75.94                               | 75.72                               | 92.36                               | 82.52                               |\n| Ablation over Cross-model Projector | Ablation over Cross-model Projector | Ablation over Cross-model Projector | Ablation over Cross-model Projector | Ablation over Cross-model Projector | Ablation over Cross-model Projector | Ablation over Cross-model Projector | Ablation over Cross-model Projector | Ablation over Cross-model Projector |\n| use MLP instead of Qformer          | 66.60                               | 64.51                               | 96.73                               | 74.41                               | 86.23                               | 83.38                               | 94.72                               | 91.92                               |\n| Ablation over Text Source           | Ablation over Text Source           | Ablation over Text Source           | Ablation over Text Source           | Ablation over Text Source           | Ablation over Text Source           | Ablation over Text Source           | Ablation over Text Source           | Ablation over Text Source           |\n| only cell metadata                  | 67.97                               | 67.54                               | 97.79                               | 80.76                               | 87.51                               | 82.15                               | 94.18                               | 91.39                               |\n| only free text                      | 67.94                               | 66.69                               | 96.56                               | 74.41                               | 83.03                               | 78.23                               | 94.83                               | 93.34                               |\n\nTable 6: Results of cell type annotation (%) on the Tabula Sapiens [65] dataset. The models are fine-tuned on a certain proportion of test cell types. Acc@N denotes top-N accuracy.\n\n|              | Zero-Shot   | Zero-Shot      | Zero-Shot     | Fine-tuned on 10% Types   | Fine-tuned on 10% Types   | Fine-tuned on 10% Types   | Fine-tuned on 20% Types   | Fine-tuned on 20% Types   | Fine-tuned on 20% Types   | Fine-tuned on 30% Types   | Fine-tuned on 30% Types   | Fine-tuned on 30% Types   |\n|--------------|-------------|----------------|---------------|---------------------------|---------------------------|---------------------------|---------------------------|---------------------------|---------------------------|---------------------------|---------------------------|---------------------------|\n| Model        | Acc@1       | Acc@5          | Acc@10        | Acc@1                     | Acc@5                     | Acc@10                    | Acc@1                     | Acc@5                     | Acc@10                    | Acc@1                     | Acc@5                     | Acc@10                    |\n| Random       | 0.6         | 3.1            | 6.2           | 0.6                       | 3.1                       | 6.2                       | 0.6                       | 3.1                       | 6.2                       | 0.6                       | 3.1                       | 6.2                       |\n| BioTranlator | -           | -              | -             | 3.5                       | 33.6                      | 45.4                      | 13.4                      | 48.2                      | 63.5                      | 13.7                      | 50.6                      | 68.6                      |\n| LangCell     | 28.6        | 69.2           | 82.9          | 30.5                      | 71.0                      | 83.7                      | 35.0                      | 74.6                      | 86.4                      | 38.2                      | 83.0                      | 92.1                      |\n| scMMGPT      | 49.1 +20    | . 5 83.1 +13 . | 8 91.1 +8 . 2 | 55.7 +25 .                | 2 89.2 +18 .              | 96.0 +12 . 3              | 59.7 +24 .                | 8 90.4 +15 .              | 8 96.8 +10                | . 4 60.9 +22              | . 7 93.6 +10              | . 6 98.4 +6 . 4           |\n\nImpact of text source. We next investigate how different textual inputs affect performance. We evaluate three variations: (1) using both cell metadata and free-text descriptions as the text source, (2) using metadata only, and (3) using free-text only. As shown in Table 5, combining metadata and free-text leads to the highest accuracy and F1-score. This highlights that structured and unstructured textual information provides complementary benefits.\n\nImpact of Model Architecture. Finally, we examine the effect of the Q-former module in capturing cross-modal interactions. We try an MLP variant of scMMGPT where the Q-former projector is replaced with simple MLP layers while keeping other components unchanged. As shown in Table 5, the original Q-former design outperforms the MLP variant across all metrics, highlighting its effectiveness in modeling fine-grained relationships between modalities.\n\n### 4.3 Out-of-Distribution Evaluation (RQ3)\n\nWe evaluate the model's cell annotation performance under out-of-distribution settings. The experiment is conducted on the Tabula Sapiens [65] dataset, which comprises 161 distinct human cell types, most absent from our pre-training corpus. We report the accuracies under fine-tuning settings, where the model is trained on different proportions of test cell types (10%, 20%, and 30%) and tested on the remaining proportions. We compare our model against two baseline methods, BioTranslator [66] and LangCell [12]. Top-N accuracies (Acc @N) are used as the metric.\n\nThe experimental results are summarized in Table 6. scMMGPT achieves an Acc@1 of 49.1% and an Acc@5 of 83.1% without further fine-tuning, surpassing all the baseline models even in a fine-tuning setting. As the proportion of fine-tuning cell types increases, scMMGPT consistently improves its performance across all metrics, reaching a maximum Acc@1 of 60.9% when fine-tuned on 30% cell types. These results demonstrate that scMMGPT's pre-trained knowledge of cellular and textual data enables strong generalization to unseen cell types.\n\n## 5 Conclusion and Future Works\n\nIn this study, we introduced scMMGPT, a groundbreaking multimodal framework designed for advanced single-cell analysis. By effectively bridging scRNA-seq data with textual information, scMMGPT supports a range of tasks, including cell type annotation, cell clustering, and cell description generation. This integration is accomplished through the synergistic use of a cell-specific PLM and a text PLM, connected by innovative cross-modal projectors. Trained on 27 million cells from the CELLxGENE dataset, scMMGPT demonstrates superior performance across diverse single-cell analysis applications.\n\nLooking forward, we aim to broaden the scope of scMMGPT by incorporating additional species and integrating further cell modalities, like scATAC-seq and CITE-seq. Such expansion will empower scMMGPT to address more challenges associated with multi-omic integration [67], cross-omic translation [68], and novel cell type discovery [10], further enhancing its utility in single-cell research.\n\n### References\n\n- [1] Antoine-Emmanuel Saliba, Alexander J Westermann, Stanislaw A Gorski, and Jörg Vogel. Single-cell rna-seq: advances and future challenges. Nucleic acids research , 42(14):8845-8860, 2014.\n- [2] Alex K Shalek, Rahul Satija, Joe Shuga, John J Trombetta, Dave Gennert, Diana Lu, Peilin Chen, Rona S Gertner, Jellert T Gaublomme, Nir Yosef, et al. Single-cell rna-seq reveals dynamic paracrine control of cellular variation. Nature , 510(7505):363-369, 2014.\n- [3] Adam D Silverman, Ashty S Karim, and Michael C Jewett. Cell-free gene expression: an expanded repertoire of applications. Nature Reviews Genetics , 21(3):151-170, 2020.\n- [4] Philipp Angerer, Lukas Simon, Sophie Tritschler, F Alexander Wolf, David Fischer, and Fabian J Theis. Single cells make big data: new challenges and opportunities in transcriptomics. Current opinion in systems biology , 4:85-91, 2017.\n- [5] Ashish Vaswani, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar, Jakob Uszkoreit, Llion Jones, Aidan N Gomez, Łukasz Kaiser, and Illia Polosukhin. Attention is all you need. Advances in neural information processing systems , 30, 2017.\n- [6] OpenAI. Gpt-4 technical report. arXiv preprint arXiv:2303.08774 , 2023.\n- [7] Hugo Touvron, Thibaut Lavril, Gautier Izacard, Xavier Martinet, Marie-Anne Lachaux, Timothée Lacroix, Baptiste Rozière, Naman Goyal, Eric Hambro, Faisal Azhar, et al. Llama: Open and efficient foundation language models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2302.13971 , 2023.\n- [8] Christina V Theodoris, Ling Xiao, Anant Chopra, Mark D Cha