./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000248_de5b21014e76/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248_de5b21014e76_docling_json_20260812_141810/debug/trace_data.json:107:            "text_content": "Figure 2: Token length distribution of gene expression sequences after tokenization with the Geneformer tokenizer.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single-panel histogram titled “Length Distribution - combined.” The x-axis is labeled “Length” and ranges roughly from 0 to just over 4000. The y-axis is labeled “Frequency” and ranges up to about 100000. Light-blue bars show a length distribution with a broad peak around 700-1100, a gradual decline through higher lengths, and a very large spike near length 4000. A red dashed vertical line marks the mean, labeled “Mean: 1843.2.”\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, molecular structures, transformations, or model interfaces are visible. The figure appears to report a quantitative distribution of sequence/text/item lengths, with the main finding being a right-skewed distribution and a prominent boundary spike near 4000.\n\nFigure 3: Token length distribution of natural language descriptions after tokenization with the Llama-3.21B-Instruct tokenizer.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel histogram titled “Tokenized natural_desc Length - combined.” It plots tokenized `natural_desc` length distributions.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis: “# tokens”, ranging roughly from 50 to 300.\n- Y-axis: “Frequency”, up to about 100,000.\n- Light green histogram bars showing a right-skewed distribution.\n- Red dashed vertical reference line at the mean.\n- Legend in the upper right: “Mean: 104.3”.\n\nFindings:\n- Most samples cluster around approximately 70-120 tokens.\n- The distribution has a long right tail extending past 200 tokens and sparsely toward 300 tokens.\n- Mean tokenized length is marked at 104.3 tokens.\n\nBiological source objects: none visible.\nTransformations/model interfaces: tokenization length analysis is implied by the title and x-axis, but no model architecture or interface is shown.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000248_de5b21014e76/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248_de5b21014e76_docling_json_20260812_141810/debug/trace_data.json:112:            "text_content": "Figure 4: Overview of the distribution of cell types in the dataset. For clarity, only the 30 most abundant categories out of 783 are shown.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a single-panel bar chart showing cell-category counts.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Y-axis label: `Count`\n- X-axis label: `Categories`\n- X-axis categories are rotated diagonally.\n- Bars are sorted descending by count and colored from blue through gray to orange/red.\n- Each bar has a numeric count label above it.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Cell types / immune or hematopoietic cell categories, including examples visible in the plot:\n  - oligodendrocyte\n  - neuron\n  - CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell\n  - T cell\n  - fibroblast\n  - granulocyte-monocyte progenitor cell\n  - CD8-positive alpha-beta T cell\n  - B cell\n  - epithelial cell\n  - classical monocyte\n  - macrophage\n  - L2/3-6 intratelencephalic projecting glutamatergic neuron\n  - naive thymus-derived CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell\n  - retinal rod cell\n  - astrocyte\n  - endothelial cell\n  - alveolar macrophage\n  - CD14-positive monocyte\n  - monocyte\n  - promyelocyte\n  - central memory CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell\n  - stromal cell\n\nTransformations / analysis:\n- Aggregation of observations by biological category.\n- Counts per category are plotted as a ranked bar chart.\n\nFindings:\n- The most frequent category is `oligodendrocyte` with 29,725 counts.\n- Next highest are `neuron` with 27,981 and `CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell` with 21,994.\n- Counts decline gradually across categories, with the smallest visible bars around 5,647-5,717 counts.\n\nFigure 5 shows a skewed distribution of the top 30 disease categories (out of 128), with normal (592,932) and COVID-19 (61,961) dominating. The high count for normal likely stems from extensive use of healthy control samples in research to establish baselines, while COVID-19's prominence reflects widespread data collection during the pandemic.\n\nFigure 6 shows a skewed distribution of the top 30 tissue categ... [truncated, total 2605 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000248_de5b21014e76/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248_de5b21014e76_docling_json_20260812_141810/debug/trace_data.json:117:            "text_content": "Figure 5: Overview of the distribution of disease categories in the dataset. For clarity, only the 30 most abundant categories out of 128 are shown.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel bar chart titled `final_combined_disease_top_values - Top 30 Categories (Log Scale)`. It shows the top 30 disease/category labels ranked by count, with the y-axis labeled `Count (Log Scale)` and the x-axis labeled `Categories`.\n\nVisible content:\n- Bar chart with rotated category labels on the x-axis.\n- Log-scaled y-axis.\n- Counts printed above each bar.\n- Highest category is `normal` with `592,932`.\n- Next largest categories include `COVID-19` with `61,961`, `Pulmonic disease` with `14,656`, `tuberculosis` with `12,811`, and `lung adenocarcinoma` with `12,005`.\n- Other visible categories include disease terms such as `squamous lung epithelium`, `malignant ovarian germ tumor`, `dementia`, `Coronary diseases`, `breast cancer`, `chronic renal disease`, `dilated cardiomyopathy`, `Alzheimer disease`, `atrial fibrillation`, `arteriosclerosis`, `myocardial infarction`, `B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia`, `Parkinson`, `pulmonary fibrosis`, `clear cell renal carcinoma`, `nonpapillary renal cell carcinoma`, `basal cell carcinoma`, `acute myeloid leukemia`, `primary sclerosing cholangitis`, `triple-negative breast carcinoma`, `benign prostatic hyperplasia`, `Sjogren’s syndrome`, `squamous cell lung carcinoma`, `Duchenne muscular dystrophy`, `chronic obstructive pulmonary disease`, and `follicular lymphoma`.\n\nBiological/medical source objects: disease and clinical category labels, likely aggregated from biomedical records or annotations.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces: no model architecture, microscopy, molecular assay, or computational workflow is visible. The only transformation shown is ranking/count aggregation with logarithmic scaling.\n\nFinding shown: the distribution is highly imbalanced, dominated by `normal`, followed by `COVID-19`, with remaining disease... [truncated, total 2032 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/july_update_2026-07-06__rec_000050_23824e963093/july_update_2026_07_06_rec_000050_23824e963093_docling_json_20260812_141419/debug/trace_data.json:52:            "text_content": "epochs on 1,000 reasoning traces in the RNA setting without CPT or RL, while G-R-C-S 8 , 1 denotes a Gemma + TranscriptFormer model adapted with CPT and then fine-tuned for 8 epochs.\n\n## 4 Results: How Training Stages Shape Biological Reasoning in LLMs\n\nWenow present our main results related to scaling post-training for biology in compute- or data-bound settings. Concrete model settings for all our experiments in this section, including context windows, input sequence lengths, and other hyperparameters can be found in the appendix.\n\nFigure 2: Supervised fine-tuning improves in-domain performance but reduces out-of-domain robustness. As SFT compute increases, ID performance continues to improve, while OOD performance peaks early and declines, indicating over-specialization to the training data. DNA/ RNA mean and std. over 3 random seeds (we only use one seed for Proteins, due to the size of the dataset).\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThree side-by-side line-plot panels compare model performance over SFT epochs.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Left panel: `DNA`\n  - Y-axis: `Accuracy`\n  - X-axis: `SFT epochs`\n  - Epoch ticks: 0, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32\n- Middle panel: `RNA`\n  - Y-axis: `Accuracy`\n  - X-axis: `SFT epochs`\n- Right panel: `Proteins`\n  - Y-axis: `F_max`\n  - X-axis: `SFT epochs`\n\nLegend/model interfaces:\n- `Qwen3-1.7B ID-Test`\n- `Qwen3-1.7B OOD-Test`\n- `Qwen3-4B ID-Test`\n- `Qwen3-4B OOD-Test`\n\nBiological source objects:\n- DNA sequence-related tasks\n- RNA sequence-related tasks\n- Protein sequence-related tasks\n\nTransformations/experimental variable:\n- Performance is plotted as a function of supervised fine-tuning duration, measured in `SFT epochs`.\n\nFindings visible in the figure:\n- For DNA and RNA, accuracy rises sharply from 0 to early epochs, then plateaus.\n- The 4B model generally reaches higher ID-Test performance than the 1.7B model.\n- OOD-Test curves are consistently lower than ID-Test curves.\n- Protein `F_max` improves up to roughly 4-8 epochs, then declines by ... [truncated, total 4919 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/july_update_2026-07-06__rec_000050_23824e963093/july_update_2026_07_06_rec_000050_23824e963093_docling_json_20260812_141419/debug/trace_data.json:62:            "text_content": "### 4.3 Continued Pre-Training Establishes the Foundation for Biological Reasoning\n\nFigure 5: Continued pre-training improves the effectiveness of downstream post-training. CPT improves both SFT and RL performance, with the largest gains appearing after RL and in out-of-domain settings.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar chart comparing model accuracy across task settings for DNA and RNA.\n\nVisible structure:\n- Four panels arranged horizontally.\n- Left two panels are grouped under the header “DNA”:\n  - “ID-test”\n  - “OOD-test”\n- Right two panels are grouped under the header “RNA”:\n  - “ID-test”\n  - “OOD-test”\n- Y-axis label: “Accuracy”, ranging approximately from 0.0 to 1.0.\n- X-axis categories in each panel:\n  - Base\n  - SFT\n  - RL\n  - CPT+SFT\n  - CPT+RL\n- Legend:\n  - Blue bars: Qwen3-1.7B\n  - Orange bars: Qwen3-4B\n\nBiological source objects:\n- DNA sequence tasks\n- RNA sequence tasks\n\nModel interfaces / transformations shown:\n- Comparison of baseline and training/adaptation strategies:\n  - Base\n  - supervised fine-tuning, SFT\n  - reinforcement learning, RL\n  - continued pretraining plus SFT, CPT+SFT\n  - continued pretraining plus RL, CPT+RL\n- Comparison across two model sizes: Qwen3-1.7B and Qwen3-4B.\n- Evaluation split comparison: in-distribution test and out-of-distribution test.\n\nVisible findings:\n- For DNA, RL and CPT+RL achieve the highest accuracies, especially on ID-test and OOD-test.\n- For RNA, Base accuracy is low, while SFT, RL, CPT+SFT, and CPT+RL substantially improve performance.\n- CPT+RL appears among the best-performing settings across DNA and RNA panels.\n- Qwen3-4B is generally similar to or slightly higher than Qwen3-1.7B in many conditions.\n\nWe next study whether continued pre-training changes how much downstream post-training can help. In the DNA and RNA settings, we first adapt the base backbones with continued pre-training on biological texts, yielding model families of the form Q1-D/R-C and Q4-D/R-C. We then eval... [truncated, total 6184 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/july_update_2026-07-06__rec_000050_23824e963093/july_update_2026_07_06_rec_000050_23824e963093_docling_json_20260812_141419/debug/trace_data.json:157:            "text_content": "- Protein RL epoch sweep : epochs ∈ { 1 , 2 , 4 , 8 } at 20% data.\n- DNA/ RNA SFT/RL epoch sweep : epochs ∈ { 1 , 2 , 4 , 8 , 16 , 32 } for SFT (capped at 8 for RL), across Qwen3-1.7B, Qwen3-4B, and their post-CPT variants.\n\n### B.7 Compute Resources and Training Budget\n\nAll training and evaluation are run on a GPU cluster using NVIDIA H100 (80 GB) and H200 (141 GB) GPUs. We use one GPU per run; multi-node training was not required for any reported result.\n\n- DNA/RNA CPT : 1 × H100, walltime up to 3 days per run. Eight runs ≈ 24 GPU-days.\n- Protein SFT : 1 × H100, walltime up to 3 days per run. Eight runs across the reported data and epoch sweeps.\n- Protein RL : 1 × H200, walltime up to 7 days per run with automatic checkpointing and requeueing. The reported warm-start β = 10 -4 sweep comprises 8 runs, corresponding to the protein RL results in Figure 4. Each training run chains two GPU evaluation jobs, one for the ID split and one for the OOD split, with sampling at T = 0 . 7 . We did not include the exploratory β = 10 -3 and no-warm-start protein RL runs in the reported results, so they are excluded from the compute accounting here.\n- DNA/RNA SFT/RL : 1 × H100, walltime up to 7 days per run. SFT and RL are submitted as chained pairs per backbone, with RL automatically selecting the best SFT checkpoint by validation accuracy.\n\nThe dominant reported compute cost is the protein RL sweep: 8 training runs at up to 7 days each ( ≈ 56 H200-days), plus 16 paired ID/OOD evaluation jobs ( ≈ 20 GPU-days). The DNA/RNA CPT and protein SFT sweeps consume the next-largest shares at ≈ 24 GPU-days each.\n\n## C Main-Results Tables\n\n### C.1 Results for Figure 2\n\n| Task    | Model      | Split   |   Base |   SFT1 |   SFT2 |   SFT4 |   SFT8 |   SFT16 |   SFT32 |\n|---------|------------|---------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|---------|---------|\n| DNA     | Qwen3-1.7B | ID      |  0.562 |  0.724 |  0.838 |  0.866 |  0.893 |   0.907 |   0.879 |\n| DNA     | Qwen3-1.7B | OO... [truncated, total 3072 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/july_update_2026-07-06__rec_000050_23824e963093/july_update_2026_07_06_rec_000050_23824e963093_docling_json_20260812_141419/debug/trace_data.json:167:            "text_content": "Table 5: Full numerical results corresponding to Figure 5 for the continued-pretraining ablation. DNA and RNA are evaluated with accuracy on the ID and OOD test splits. The Base column reports the non-CPT backbone before task-specific post-training. SFT and SFT+RL report the strongest non-CPT post-training configurations, while CPT+SFT and CPT+SFT+RL report the corresponding configurations initialized from the CPT-adapted backbone.\n\n| Task   | Model      | Split   |   Base |   SFT |   SFT+RL |   CPT+SFT |   CPT+SFT+RL |\n|--------|------------|---------|--------|-------|----------|-----------|--------------|\n| DNA    | Qwen3-1.7B | ID      |  0.721 | 0.905 |    0.939 |     0.835 |        0.965 |\n| DNA    | Qwen3-1.7B | OOD     |  0.651 | 0.724 |    0.893 |     0.935 |        0.959 |\n| DNA    | Qwen3-4B   | ID      |  0.798 | 0.894 |    0.980 |     0.917 |        0.986 |\n| DNA    | Qwen3-4B   | OOD     |  0.668 | 0.753 |    0.917 |     0.924 |        0.970 |\n| RNA    | Qwen3-1.7B | ID      |  0.209 | 0.890 |    0.773 |     0.902 |        0.936 |\n| RNA    | Qwen3-1.7B | OOD     |  0.181 | 0.569 |    0.692 |     0.718 |        0.754 |\n| RNA    | Qwen3-4B   | ID      |  0.218 | 0.912 |    0.918 |     0.910 |        0.939 |\n| RNA    | Qwen3-4B   | OOD     |  0.197 | 0.588 |    0.744 |     0.732 |        0.765 |\n\n### C.5 Results for Figure 6\n\nTable 6: Full numerical results corresponding to Figure 6 for the Gemma4-E2B RNA backbone ablation. Metrics are accuracy on the RNA ID and OOD test splits. The SFT rows report the supervised fine-tuning epoch sweep, including the base model at epoch 0. The RL rows report the reinforcement-learning epoch sweep initialized from the strongest SFT checkpoint; RL was evaluated through 16 epochs.\n\n| Stage   | Split   |   Base |   Epoch 1 |   Epoch 2 |   Epoch 4 |   Epoch 8 |   Epoch 16 | Epoch 32   |\n|---------|---------|--------|-----------|-----------|-----------|-----------|------------|------------|\n| SFT     | ID      |  0.223 |     0.... [truncated, total 5241 chars]",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1/full_2026_07_06_rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1_docling_json_20260812_134529/docling/document.dclg:11004:<![CDATA[As C2S-Scale is a multimodal foundation model designed to process and reason over both large-scale transcriptomic data and rich textual corpora, its pretraining utilizes a greater number of FLOPs compared to specialized, expressiononly foundation models. For instance, the C2S-Scale-Gemma-2 2B model uses a higher overall FLOP count than scGPT. This increased compute is fundamental to C2S-Scale's ability to span an unprecedented range of single-cell tasks-including perturbation prediction, cell-type annotation, and complex natural language interpretation-all within a single, unified architecture. The resulting performance gains, which scale consistently with increasing FLOPs and model size (Figure 2), validate this strategic investment in a comprehensive, multimodal foundation model. We show that the superior performance of C2S-Scale across the entire spectrum of prediction, generation, and reasoning tasks is a direct consequence of scaling both the model size and the complexity of the integrated (transcriptomic and textual) training data, positioning it as a robust next-generation platform for biological discovery.]]>  </text>
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1/full_2026_07_06_rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1_docling_json_20260812_134529/docling/document.dclg:11243:      Figure 2: Caption continued on following page.
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1/full_2026_07_06_rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1_docling_json_20260812_134529/docling/document.dclg:11411:<![CDATA[Figure 5: C2S-Scale demonstrates superior single-cell question answering performance compared to state-of-the-art (SOTA) LLMs. A, Example QA scenario based on scRNA-seq data. B, Empirical comparison of C2S-Scale and SOTA LLMs on single-cell QA tasks, highlighting C2S-Scale's advantage in domain-specific reasoning. Error bars represent standard deviation across test set QA samples. C, Overview of the GRPO framework, which further refines model performance by training on preference data.]]>    </caption>
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1/full_2026_07_06_rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1_docling_json_20260812_134529/docling/document.dclg:13323:<![CDATA[Figure 20: The 97 genes in the 'Hallmark Interferon Alpha Response' gene set, used as a proxy for estimating the level of baseline interferon activity for virtual screening.]]>    </caption>
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_03/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1/full_2026_07_06_rec_000090_0d9defcc25c1_docling_json_20260812_134529/docling/document.dclg:13350:<![CDATA[Figure 21: The 381 genes in the 'Reactome Class I MHC-Mediated Antigen Processing Presentation' gene set, used to score virtual screen outputs.]]>  </text>
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001617_76b0056d59c3/full_2026_07_06_rec_001617_76b0056d59c3_docling_json_20260812_135401/docling/chunks.json:535:    "text": "3 CellPuzzles : Batch-Level Reasoning for Cell Type Annotation\nIn real-world single-cell analysis, cell type annotation is rarely performed on individual cells in isolation. Instead, it typically occurs at the batch level , where groups of cells from the same donor or sample are jointly analyzed. Experts commonly cluster cells based on gene expression profiles, identify representative marker genes for each cluster, and assign labels by integrating both expression patterns and contextual metadata such as tissue origin and disease status.\nFigure 2: CellPuzzles formulates cell type annotation as a batch-level reasoning task that integrates gene expression and contextual metadata, inspired by how experts annotate cells in practice.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic comparison of two cell type annotation workflows with four main panel regions arranged in two rows.\n\nTop row: A human expert task workflow. The left “Input” panel shows clustered single-cell data with colored clusters labeled Cluster 2, Cluster 3, Cluster 4, and Cluster N, each associated with marker gene lists such as “SFTPC, SFTPA1, SFTPB, NAPSA, ...”, “C1QA, APOE, MARCO, LYZ, ...”, and “IGHM, MZB1, JCHAIN, XBP1, ...”. The center panel labeled “Human Expert Task” shows an expert assigning a cell type label to each cluster using representative marker genes, contextual metadata, biological knowledge, and reference sources. The right “Output” panel lists assigned cell types including Pulmonary Alveolar Type 2 (AT2) Cells, CD8+ Cytotoxic T Cells, Lung Pericyte, Alveolar Macrophages, and Plasma Cells.\n\nBottom row: A “CellPuzzles Task” workflow. The left “Input” panel shows individual cells from clusters, with representative sampled cells labeled [Cell 1], [Cell 2], [Cell 3], [Cell 4], and [Cell N], each paired with gene lists such as “S100A9, TMSB10, RPL37, ...”, “MALAT1, FTL, B2M, ...”, “MALAT1, FTL, AKR1B10, ...”, “B2M, MT2A, TMSB4X, ...”, and “IGLC3, IGLC2, IGHM, ...”. Arrows indicate selected representative cells from clusters. The center panel labeled “CellPuzzles Task” shows a model/interface labeled “Cell-o1” receiving N cells, contextual metadata, and N candidate cell types, then reasoning to determine the optimal label assignment and provide reasoning traces. The right “Output” panel shows colored cell-to-label assignment lines connecting cells to candidate labels including CD4-positive, alpha-beta T Cell; CD8-positive, alpha-beta T Cell; Smooth Muscle Cell; Lung Macrophage; Non-classical Monocyte; Capillary Endothelial Cell; Plasma Cell; and Respiratory Basal Cell.\n\nBiological source objects include single-cell clusters, individual cells, marker genes, and immune/lung-related cell type labels. The figure depicts a transformation from marker gene or cell-level input data to annotated biological cell type outputs, comparing expert manual annotation with an automated CellPuzzles/Cell-o1 reasoning task.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001617_76b0056d59c3/full_2026_07_06_rec_001617_76b0056d59c3_docling_json_20260812_135401/docling/chunks.json:612:    "text": "3 CellPuzzles : Batch-Level Reasoning for Cell Type Annotation\nTo emulate this expert-driven process, we introduce CellPuzzles , a novel benchmark shown in Figure 2 that formulates cell type annotation as a batch-level reasoning task. Formally, each instance consists of a batch of N cells C = { c 1 , c 2 , . . . , c N } , each sampled from a distinct cell type within the same donor and experimental batch. Each cell c i serves as a proxy for a cluster centroid and is represented by a ranked list of its top M expressed genes g i = [ g i 1 , g i 2 , . . . , g iM ] , which approximate the differentially expressed genes used by experts to define and annotate single cell clusters.\nThe entire batch is associated with a contextual description m , derived from donor-level metadata such as tissue type, disease status, sex, developmental stage, and other biologically relevant attributes when available. We also provide a candidate label set Y = { y 1 , y 2 , . . . , y N } , which contains the ground-truth cell types for the N cells in the batch, randomly shuffled to remove positional biases. This setup mirrors the expert annotation scenario, where cell types are selected from a biologically plausible candidate pool rather than freely generated. The objective is to learn a mapping f : C → Y that assigns each cell to a unique label in Y by jointly considering gene expression profiles and contextual metadata, while generating interpretable reasoning traces to justify the label assignments.\nAs illustrated in Figure 3, CellPuzzles is built from human single-cell datasets in Cell × Gene portal [39] that span diverse tissues, disease conditions, and donor demographics. The detailed dataset construction protocol is provided in the Supplementary Materials.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001617_76b0056d59c3/full_2026_07_06_rec_001617_76b0056d59c3_docling_json_20260812_135401/docling/chunks.json:1833:    "text": "6 Analysis and Discussion\n6.1 Training and Inference Dynamics under Different Configurations\nTo understand how different training strategies affect model performance and behavior, we compare four training configurations that differ along three dimensions: the presence of SFT initialization, the RL algorithm (GRPO vs. PPO (Proximal Policy Optimization) [43]), and the design of the reward function. As shown in Figure 5, Cell-o1 (A) with SFT, GRPO, and batch-level reward achieves stable training, controlled response length, and the best test performance. The response length fluctuates briefly at the beginning of training and then stabilizes, indicating that the model learns to balance output structure and reward optimization during training. Removing SFT initialization (B) leads to ineffective reward learning and a sharp increase in response length, indicating poor task alignment. Replacing GRPO with PPO (C) results in noisier reward trajectories and limited test performance gains, likely due to a mismatch between the reward objective and the final evaluation metric. The steadily declining response length suggests that PPO encourages shorter, less informative outputs to stabilize rewards, potentially at the expense of reasoning quality. Configuration (D) augments the reward with a cell-level accuracy component, using the average of cell and batch-level accuracy as the training signal. This leads to higher training reward scores, but slightly less stable test performance, which remains overall comparable to configuration (A).",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001617_76b0056d59c3/full_2026_07_06_rec_001617_76b0056d59c3_docling_json_20260812_135401/docling/chunks.json:1891:    "text": "6 Analysis and Discussion\n6.1 Training and Inference Dynamics under Different Configurations\nFigure 5: Comparing training and inference dynamics across different configurations.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific plot with four labeled conditions: **(A)**, **(B)**, **(C)**, and **(D)**. Each condition contains two stacked line charts.\n\nVisible content:\n- Top row plots **Training Reward** in red on the left y-axis and **Response Length** in blue on the right y-axis, across training steps or iterations on the x-axis.\n- Bottom row plots **Test Performance** in red on the left y-axis and **Response Length** in blue on the right y-axis.\n- Each panel includes a small legend-like text box specifying:\n  - **Base: w/ SFT**\n  - **RL: GRPO** or **RL: PPO**\n  - **Reward: Batch Reward** or **Reward: Cell & Batch Reward**\n\nPanel details:\n- **(A)**: Base with SFT, RL method GRPO, reward Batch Reward. Training reward increases gradually; response length fluctuates around a high range. Test performance rises gradually.\n- **(B)**: Base with no SFT, RL method GRPO, reward Batch Reward. Training reward remains near zero; response length increases sharply late in training. Test performance stays near zero.\n- **(C)**: Base with SFT, RL method PPO, reward Batch Reward. Training reward is noisy and variable; response length decreases over time. Test performance remains relatively flat around ~0.18–0.20.\n- **(D)**: Base with SFT, RL method GRPO, reward Cell & Batch Reward. Training reward increases to the highest visible level among panels; response length stays moderately high. Test performance increases overall with fluctuations.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, anatomical structures, cells, tissues, or molecular diagrams are visible. The figure appears to report machine learning or reinforcement learning training outcomes, comparing model/interface conditions involving SFT, GRPO, PPO, reward design, training reward, test performance, and generated response length.",
