./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001617_76b0056d59c3/full_2026_07_06_rec_001617_76b0056d59c3_docling_json_20260812_135401/docling_graph/provenance.json:548:      "text": "3 CellPuzzles : Batch-Level Reasoning for Cell Type Annotation\nIn real-world single-cell analysis, cell type annotation is rarely performed on individual cells in isolation. Instead, it typically occurs at the batch level , where groups of cells from the same donor or sample are jointly analyzed. Experts commonly cluster cells based on gene expression profiles, identify representative marker genes for each cluster, and assign labels by integrating both expression patterns and contextual metadata such as tissue origin and disease status.\nFigure 2: CellPuzzles formulates cell type annotation as a batch-level reasoning task that integrates gene expression and contextual metadata, inspired by how experts annotate cells in practice.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic comparison of two cell type annotation workflows with four main panel regions arranged in two rows.\n\nTop row: A human expert task workflow. The left “Input” panel shows clustered single-cell data with colored clusters labeled Cluster 2, Cluster 3, Cluster 4, and Cluster N, each associated with marker gene lists such as “SFTPC, SFTPA1, SFTPB, NAPSA, ...”, “C1QA, APOE, MARCO, LYZ, ...”, and “IGHM, MZB1, JCHAIN, XBP1, ...”. The center panel labeled “Human Expert Task” shows an expert assigning a cell type label to each cluster using representative marker genes, contextual metadata, biological knowledge, and reference sources. The right “Output” panel lists assigned cell types including Pulmonary Alveolar Type 2 (AT2) Cells, CD8+ Cytotoxic T Cells, Lung Pericyte, Alveolar Macrophages, and Plasma Cells.\n\nBottom row: A “CellPuzzles Task” workflow. The left “Input” panel shows individual cells from clusters, with representative sampled cells labeled [Cell 1], [Cell 2], [Cell 3], [Cell 4], and [Cell N], each paired with gene lists such as “S100A9, TMSB10, RPL37, ...”, “MALAT1, FTL, B2M, ...”, “MALAT1, FTL, AKR1B10, ...”, “B2M, MT2A, TMSB4X, ...”, and “IGLC3, IGLC2, IGHM, ...”. Arrows indicate selected representative cells from clusters. The center panel labeled “CellPuzzles Task” shows a model/interface labeled “Cell-o1” receiving N cells, contextual metadata, and N candidate cell types, then reasoning to determine the optimal label assignment and provide reasoning traces. The right “Output” panel shows colored cell-to-label assignment lines connecting cells to candidate labels including CD4-positive, alpha-beta T Cell; CD8-positive, alpha-beta T Cell; Smooth Muscle Cell; Lung Macrophage; Non-classical Monocyte; Capillary Endothelial Cell; Plasma Cell; and Respiratory Basal Cell.\n\nBiological source objects include single-cell clusters, individual cells, marker genes, and immune/lung-related cell type labels. The figure depicts a transformation from marker gene or cell-level input data to annotated biological cell type outputs, comparing expert manual annotation with an automated CellPuzzles/Cell-o1 reasoning task.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001617_76b0056d59c3/full_2026_07_06_rec_001617_76b0056d59c3_docling_json_20260812_135401/docling_graph/provenance.json:625:      "text": "3 CellPuzzles : Batch-Level Reasoning for Cell Type Annotation\nTo emulate this expert-driven process, we introduce CellPuzzles , a novel benchmark shown in Figure 2 that formulates cell type annotation as a batch-level reasoning task. Formally, each instance consists of a batch of N cells C = { c 1 , c 2 , . . . , c N } , each sampled from a distinct cell type within the same donor and experimental batch. Each cell c i serves as a proxy for a cluster centroid and is represented by a ranked list of its top M expressed genes g i = [ g i 1 , g i 2 , . . . , g iM ] , which approximate the differentially expressed genes used by experts to define and annotate single cell clusters.\nThe entire batch is associated with a contextual description m , derived from donor-level metadata such as tissue type, disease status, sex, developmental stage, and other biologically relevant attributes when available. We also provide a candidate label set Y = { y 1 , y 2 , . . . , y N } , which contains the ground-truth cell types for the N cells in the batch, randomly shuffled to remove positional biases. This setup mirrors the expert annotation scenario, where cell types are selected from a biologically plausible candidate pool rather than freely generated. The objective is to learn a mapping f : C → Y that assigns each cell to a unique label in Y by jointly considering gene expression profiles and contextual metadata, while generating interpretable reasoning traces to justify the label assignments.\nAs illustrated in Figure 3, CellPuzzles is built from human single-cell datasets in Cell × Gene portal [39] that span diverse tissues, disease conditions, and donor demographics. The detailed dataset construction protocol is provided in the Supplementary Materials.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001617_76b0056d59c3/full_2026_07_06_rec_001617_76b0056d59c3_docling_json_20260812_135401/docling_graph/provenance.json:1846:      "text": "6 Analysis and Discussion\n6.1 Training and Inference Dynamics under Different Configurations\nTo understand how different training strategies affect model performance and behavior, we compare four training configurations that differ along three dimensions: the presence of SFT initialization, the RL algorithm (GRPO vs. PPO (Proximal Policy Optimization) [43]), and the design of the reward function. As shown in Figure 5, Cell-o1 (A) with SFT, GRPO, and batch-level reward achieves stable training, controlled response length, and the best test performance. The response length fluctuates briefly at the beginning of training and then stabilizes, indicating that the model learns to balance output structure and reward optimization during training. Removing SFT initialization (B) leads to ineffective reward learning and a sharp increase in response length, indicating poor task alignment. Replacing GRPO with PPO (C) results in noisier reward trajectories and limited test performance gains, likely due to a mismatch between the reward objective and the final evaluation metric. The steadily declining response length suggests that PPO encourages shorter, less informative outputs to stabilize rewards, potentially at the expense of reasoning quality. Configuration (D) augments the reward with a cell-level accuracy component, using the average of cell and batch-level accuracy as the training signal. This leads to higher training reward scores, but slightly less stable test performance, which remains overall comparable to configuration (A).",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_001617_76b0056d59c3/full_2026_07_06_rec_001617_76b0056d59c3_docling_json_20260812_135401/docling_graph/provenance.json:1904:      "text": "6 Analysis and Discussion\n6.1 Training and Inference Dynamics under Different Configurations\nFigure 5: Comparing training and inference dynamics across different configurations.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific plot with four labeled conditions: **(A)**, **(B)**, **(C)**, and **(D)**. Each condition contains two stacked line charts.\n\nVisible content:\n- Top row plots **Training Reward** in red on the left y-axis and **Response Length** in blue on the right y-axis, across training steps or iterations on the x-axis.\n- Bottom row plots **Test Performance** in red on the left y-axis and **Response Length** in blue on the right y-axis.\n- Each panel includes a small legend-like text box specifying:\n  - **Base: w/ SFT**\n  - **RL: GRPO** or **RL: PPO**\n  - **Reward: Batch Reward** or **Reward: Cell & Batch Reward**\n\nPanel details:\n- **(A)**: Base with SFT, RL method GRPO, reward Batch Reward. Training reward increases gradually; response length fluctuates around a high range. Test performance rises gradually.\n- **(B)**: Base with no SFT, RL method GRPO, reward Batch Reward. Training reward remains near zero; response length increases sharply late in training. Test performance stays near zero.\n- **(C)**: Base with SFT, RL method PPO, reward Batch Reward. Training reward is noisy and variable; response length decreases over time. Test performance remains relatively flat around ~0.18–0.20.\n- **(D)**: Base with SFT, RL method GRPO, reward Cell & Batch Reward. Training reward increases to the highest visible level among panels; response length stays moderately high. Test performance increases overall with fluctuations.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, anatomical structures, cells, tissues, or molecular diagrams are visible. The figure appears to report machine learning or reinforcement learning training outcomes, comparing model/interface conditions involving SFT, GRPO, PPO, reward design, training reward, test performance, and generated response length.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_215730/docling/document.json:2335:      "orig": "Figure 2: Detailed overview of the Cell2Sentence framework. Single-cell gene expression profiles are transformed into cell sentences via expression rank orderering of gene names. Cell sentences may be annotated with biological metadata, such as cell type, tissue, or disease. LLMs are then fine-tuned on the cell sentences. Inference is done by generating cells via autoregressive cell completion, generating cells from text, or generating text from cells. The resulting generated cell sentences can be converted back to gene expression.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_215730/docling/document.json:2336:      "text": "Figure 2: Detailed overview of the Cell2Sentence framework. Single-cell gene expression profiles are transformed into cell sentences via expression rank orderering of gene names. Cell sentences may be annotated with biological metadata, such as cell type, tissue, or disease. LLMs are then fine-tuned on the cell sentences. Inference is done by generating cells via autoregressive cell completion, generating cells from text, or generating text from cells. The resulting generated cell sentences can be converted back to gene expression."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_215730/docling/document.json:2634:      "orig": "LLMs trained on cell sentences show healthy convergence behavior. We find that infusing sequential structure to single-cell data yields a non-trivial and meaningful textual modality. We train four GPT-2 models [26] on a corpus of cell sentences sampled from [39]. While we pretrain models exclusively on cell sentences, we also experiment with fine-tuning pretrained GPT-2 models. We find that all our models learn cell sentence distributions and converge during training with a steadily decreasing perplexity (see Figure 5). In both settings, we find that larger models achieve lower perplexities. Overall, we demonstrate the capability of causal language models to learn the cell sentence semantic distribution from a narrow scRNA-seq dataset.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_215730/docling/document.json:2635:      "text": "LLMs trained on cell sentences show healthy convergence behavior. We find that infusing sequential structure to single-cell data yields a non-trivial and meaningful textual modality. We train four GPT-2 models [26] on a corpus of cell sentences sampled from [39]. While we pretrain models exclusively on cell sentences, we also experiment with fine-tuning pretrained GPT-2 models. We find that all our models learn cell sentence distributions and converge during training with a steadily decreasing perplexity (see Figure 5). In both settings, we find that larger models achieve lower perplexities. Overall, we demonstrate the capability of causal language models to learn the cell sentence semantic distribution from a narrow scRNA-seq dataset."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_215730/docling/document.json:3745:      "orig": "We train a Byte Pair Encoding (BPE) tokenizer [51] on the full cell sentence dataset, including NL prompts and cell type labels, yielding a vocabulary of 9,609 tokens. The training set contains approximately 30 million tokens, averaging 740 tokens per example. Due to the smaller embedding space, the initialized models contain slightly fewer parameters than their counterparts pretrained on a vocabulary of 50,257 tokens (93M for the small model and 313M for medium model, as shown in Figure 5). The resulting corpus exhibits sparse NL tokens due to short and repetitive prompts. Despite instruction corpora being traditionally used to fine-tune pretrained models for question answering tasks, we adopt this setting during pretraining to mirror our fine-tuning setup described in Section 3.2.3. We hypothesize that the semantic variability from prompting patterns might implicitly regularize token and positional embeddings, with natural language tokens acting as class tokens.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_215730/docling/document.json:3746:      "text": "We train a Byte Pair Encoding (BPE) tokenizer [51] on the full cell sentence dataset, including NL prompts and cell type labels, yielding a vocabulary of 9,609 tokens. The training set contains approximately 30 million tokens, averaging 740 tokens per example. Due to the smaller embedding space, the initialized models contain slightly fewer parameters than their counterparts pretrained on a vocabulary of 50,257 tokens (93M for the small model and 313M for medium model, as shown in Figure 5). The resulting corpus exhibits sparse NL tokens due to short and repetitive prompts. Despite instruction corpora being traditionally used to fine-tune pretrained models for question answering tasks, we adopt this setting during pretraining to mirror our fine-tuning setup described in Section 3.2.3. We hypothesize that the semantic variability from prompting patterns might implicitly regularize token and positional embeddings, with natural language tokens acting as class tokens."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_215730/docling/document.json:3826:      "orig": "Figure 5: Perplexity curves for finetuned and pretrained models. (A) Estimated model perplexity computed after the training loss. (B) Estimated model perplexity computed on the validation set during training. All models preserve the default GPT-2 context length of 1024 tokens.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/classification_dense/shard_02/records/full_2026-07-06__rec_003206_211e7d00b696/full_2026_07_06_rec_003206_211e7d00b696_docling_json_20260812_215730/docling/document.json:3827:      "text": "Figure 5: Perplexity curves for finetuned and pretrained models. (A) Estimated model perplexity computed after the training loss. (B) Estimated model perplexity computed on the validation set during training. All models preserve the default GPT-2 context length of 1024 tokens."
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000248_de5b21014e76/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248_de5b21014e76_docling_json_20260812_141810/docling_graph/provenance.json:3380:      "text": "B DATASET STATISTICS AND DISTRIBUTIONS\nFigure 2 depicts the token length distribution of gene expression sequences post-tokenization with the Geneformer tokenizer, averaging 1843.2 tokens. The distribution peaks between 1000-1500 tokens, tapering off, with a spike at the 4096-token maximum, indicating some sequences are adjusted to this limit.\nFigure 3 illustrates the token length distribution of natural language descriptions tokenized with the Llama3.2-1B-Instruct tokenizer, with a mean length of 104.3 tokens. The distribution peaks around 100-150 tokens and decreases steadily, with fewer descriptions exceeding 200 tokens.\nFigure 4 shows a skewed distribution of cell types, with glial cells like oligodendrocytes dominating due to abundant brain-derived data, while immune cells like T and B cells are also prominent, reflecting bias toward easily accessible lymphoid tissues. Rarer types, such as stromal and plasma cells, are underrepresented, likely due to challenges in cell isolation and lower natural prevalence, highlighting how dataset composition reflects methodological biases rather than just biology.\nFigure 2: Token length distribution of gene expression sequences after tokenization with the Geneformer tokenizer.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single-panel histogram titled “Length Distribution - combined.” The x-axis is labeled “Length” and ranges roughly from 0 to just over 4000. The y-axis is labeled “Frequency” and ranges up to about 100000. Light-blue bars show a length distribution with a broad peak around 700-1100, a gradual decline through higher lengths, and a very large spike near length 4000. A red dashed vertical line marks the mean, labeled “Mean: 1843.2.”\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, molecular structures, transformations, or model interfaces are visible. The figure appears to report a quantitative distribution of sequence/text/item lengths, with the main finding being a right-skewed distribution and a prominent boundary spike near 4000.\nFigure 3: Token length distribution of natural language descriptions after tokenization with the Llama-3.21B-Instruct tokenizer.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel histogram titled “Tokenized natural_desc Length - combined.” It plots tokenized `natural_desc` length distributions.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis: “# tokens”, ranging roughly from 50 to 300.\n- Y-axis: “Frequency”, up to about 100,000.\n- Light green histogram bars showing a right-skewed distribution.\n- Red dashed vertical reference line at the mean.\n- Legend in the upper right: “Mean: 104.3”.\n\nFindings:\n- Most samples cluster around approximately 70-120 tokens.\n- The distribution has a long right tail extending past 200 tokens and sparsely toward 300 tokens.\n- Mean tokenized length is marked at 104.3 tokens.\n\nBiological source objects: none visible.\nTransformations/model interfaces: tokenization length analysis is implied by the title and x-axis, but no model architecture or interface is shown.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000248_de5b21014e76/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248_de5b21014e76_docling_json_20260812_141810/docling_graph/provenance.json:3494:      "text": "B DATASET STATISTICS AND DISTRIBUTIONS\nFigure 4: Overview of the distribution of cell types in the dataset. For clarity, only the 30 most abundant categories out of 783 are shown.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a single-panel bar chart showing cell-category counts.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Y-axis label: `Count`\n- X-axis label: `Categories`\n- X-axis categories are rotated diagonally.\n- Bars are sorted descending by count and colored from blue through gray to orange/red.\n- Each bar has a numeric count label above it.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Cell types / immune or hematopoietic cell categories, including examples visible in the plot:\n  - oligodendrocyte\n  - neuron\n  - CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell\n  - T cell\n  - fibroblast\n  - granulocyte-monocyte progenitor cell\n  - CD8-positive alpha-beta T cell\n  - B cell\n  - epithelial cell\n  - classical monocyte\n  - macrophage\n  - L2/3-6 intratelencephalic projecting glutamatergic neuron\n  - naive thymus-derived CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell\n  - retinal rod cell\n  - astrocyte\n  - endothelial cell\n  - alveolar macrophage\n  - CD14-positive monocyte\n  - monocyte\n  - promyelocyte\n  - central memory CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell\n  - stromal cell\n\nTransformations / analysis:\n- Aggregation of observations by biological category.\n- Counts per category are plotted as a ranked bar chart.\n\nFindings:\n- The most frequent category is `oligodendrocyte` with 29,725 counts.\n- Next highest are `neuron` with 27,981 and `CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell` with 21,994.\n- Counts decline gradually across categories, with the smallest visible bars around 5,647-5,717 counts.\nFigure 5 shows a skewed distribution of the top 30 disease categories (out of 128), with normal (592,932) and COVID-19 (61,961) dominating. The high count for normal likely stems from extensive use of healthy control samples in research to establish baselines, while COVID-19's prominence reflects widespread data collection during the pandemic.\nFigure 6 shows a skewed distribution of the top 30 tissue categories (out of 347), with brain (211,063) and blood (177,460) leading, likely due to extensive sampling in neurological and hematological research. Tissues like lung (87,784) and eye (59,430) follow, reflecting biases toward accessible or clinically relevant sources.\nFigure 7 shows a skewed distribution of assay categories, with 10x v3 (480,361) and 10x v2 (242,493) dominating, likely due to their widespread adoption in droplet-based single-cell RNA sequencing. Assays like scRNA-seq3 (86,205) and 10x transcription profiling (53,623) follow, reflecting a bias toward scalable, standardized protocols.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000248_de5b21014e76/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248_de5b21014e76_docling_json_20260812_141810/docling_graph/provenance.json:3551:      "text": "B DATASET STATISTICS AND DISTRIBUTIONS\nFigure 5: Overview of the distribution of disease categories in the dataset. For clarity, only the 30 most abundant categories out of 128 are shown.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel bar chart titled `final_combined_disease_top_values - Top 30 Categories (Log Scale)`. It shows the top 30 disease/category labels ranked by count, with the y-axis labeled `Count (Log Scale)` and the x-axis labeled `Categories`.\n\nVisible content:\n- Bar chart with rotated category labels on the x-axis.\n- Log-scaled y-axis.\n- Counts printed above each bar.\n- Highest category is `normal` with `592,932`.\n- Next largest categories include `COVID-19` with `61,961`, `Pulmonic disease` with `14,656`, `tuberculosis` with `12,811`, and `lung adenocarcinoma` with `12,005`.\n- Other visible categories include disease terms such as `squamous lung epithelium`, `malignant ovarian germ tumor`, `dementia`, `Coronary diseases`, `breast cancer`, `chronic renal disease`, `dilated cardiomyopathy`, `Alzheimer disease`, `atrial fibrillation`, `arteriosclerosis`, `myocardial infarction`, `B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia`, `Parkinson`, `pulmonary fibrosis`, `clear cell renal carcinoma`, `nonpapillary renal cell carcinoma`, `basal cell carcinoma`, `acute myeloid leukemia`, `primary sclerosing cholangitis`, `triple-negative breast carcinoma`, `benign prostatic hyperplasia`, `Sjogren’s syndrome`, `squamous cell lung carcinoma`, `Duchenne muscular dystrophy`, `chronic obstructive pulmonary disease`, and `follicular lymphoma`.\n\nBiological/medical source objects: disease and clinical category labels, likely aggregated from biomedical records or annotations.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces: no model architecture, microscopy, molecular assay, or computational workflow is visible. The only transformation shown is ranking/count aggregation with logarithmic scaling.\n\nFinding shown: the distribution is highly imbalanced, dominated by `normal`, followed by `COVID-19`, with remaining disease categories much lower in count.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/july_update_2026-07-06__rec_000050_23824e963093/july_update_2026_07_06_rec_000050_23824e963093_docling_json_20260812_141419/docling_graph/provenance.json:1235:      "text": "4 Results: How Training Stages Shape Biological Reasoning in LLMs\nWenow present our main results related to scaling post-training for biology in compute- or data-bound settings. Concrete model settings for all our experiments in this section, including context windows, input sequence lengths, and other hyperparameters can be found in the appendix.\nFigure 2: Supervised fine-tuning improves in-domain performance but reduces out-of-domain robustness. As SFT compute increases, ID performance continues to improve, while OOD performance peaks early and declines, indicating over-specialization to the training data. DNA/ RNA mean and std. over 3 random seeds (we only use one seed for Proteins, due to the size of the dataset).\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThree side-by-side line-plot panels compare model performance over SFT epochs.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Left panel: `DNA`\n  - Y-axis: `Accuracy`\n  - X-axis: `SFT epochs`\n  - Epoch ticks: 0, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32\n- Middle panel: `RNA`\n  - Y-axis: `Accuracy`\n  - X-axis: `SFT epochs`\n- Right panel: `Proteins`\n  - Y-axis: `F_max`\n  - X-axis: `SFT epochs`\n\nLegend/model interfaces:\n- `Qwen3-1.7B ID-Test`\n- `Qwen3-1.7B OOD-Test`\n- `Qwen3-4B ID-Test`\n- `Qwen3-4B OOD-Test`\n\nBiological source objects:\n- DNA sequence-related tasks\n- RNA sequence-related tasks\n- Protein sequence-related tasks\n\nTransformations/experimental variable:\n- Performance is plotted as a function of supervised fine-tuning duration, measured in `SFT epochs`.\n\nFindings visible in the figure:\n- For DNA and RNA, accuracy rises sharply from 0 to early epochs, then plateaus.\n- The 4B model generally reaches higher ID-Test performance than the 1.7B model.\n- OOD-Test curves are consistently lower than ID-Test curves.\n- Protein `F_max` improves up to roughly 4-8 epochs, then declines by 16-32 epochs, especially for OOD-Test curves.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/july_update_2026-07-06__rec_000050_23824e963093/july_update_2026_07_06_rec_000050_23824e963093_docling_json_20260812_141419/docling_graph/provenance.json:1312:      "text": "4 Results: How Training Stages Shape Biological Reasoning in LLMs\n4.1 Supervised Fine-Tuning Increases Accuracy but Narrows Generalization\nWe begin by studying how supervised fine-tuning scales in biological reasoning models using pretrained Qwen3-1.7B and Qwen3-4B backbones [75].\nFixed data, variable compute. We first consider a data-constrained regime based on the DNA and RNA tasks. For each backbone, we train model families of the form Q1-D-S { 1 , 2 , 4 , 8 , 16 , 32 } , 1 , Q4-D-S { 1 , 2 , 4 , 8 , 16 , 32 } , 1 , Q1-R-S { 1 , 2 , 4 , 8 , 16 , 32 } , 1 , and Q4-R-S { 1 , 2 , 4 , 8 , 16 , 32 } , 1 , where the subscript indicates the number of SFT epochs and the use of the full available training set in each domain. We then evaluate both in-domain and out-of-domain performance. Figure 2 reveals a generalization trade-off induced by supervised fine-tuning. The amount of training that maximizes ID performance is consistently larger than the amount that maximizes OOD performance, indicating that continued fine-tuning improves fit to the training distribution after OOD generalization has already peaked. In DNA, for example, Q1-D-S improves its ID accuracy from roughly 0 . 68 at 1 epoch to about 0 . 90 by 16 epochs, while its OOD accuracy peaks much earlier, around 0 . 73 at 2-4 epochs, and then declines to about 0 . 68 by 32 epochs. The same pattern appears for the larger Q4-D-S model.\nThe RNA setting shows the same trend. Q1-R-S gains nearly 0 . 3 ID accuracy points from 1 to 4 epochs, but while its OOD performance also improves, it comes within at most 0 . 3 of ID accuracy. For Q4-R-S, ID accuracy rises from roughly 0 . 78 at 1 epoch to about 0 . 91 by 16 epochs, whereas OOD accuracy peaks much earlier, around 4 epochs, and then drifts downward by the end of training. This same pattern persists when more supervision is available: on the proteins task, Q1P-S { 1 , 2 , 4 , 8 , 16 , 32 } , 20 and Q4-P-S { 1 , 2 , 4 , 8 , 16 , 32 } , 20 both continue to improve ID F max through about 8 epochs, but OOD F max peaks earlier and then declines. More SFT compute therefore improves in-domain accuracy more reliably than it improves biological generalization.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/july_update_2026-07-06__rec_000050_23824e963093/july_update_2026_07_06_rec_000050_23824e963093_docling_json_20260812_141419/docling_graph/provenance.json:1660:      "text": "4 Results: How Training Stages Shape Biological Reasoning in LLMs\n4.3 Continued Pre-Training Establishes the Foundation for Biological Reasoning\nFigure 5: Continued pre-training improves the effectiveness of downstream post-training. CPT improves both SFT and RL performance, with the largest gains appearing after RL and in out-of-domain settings.\n\nSCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel bar chart comparing model accuracy across task settings for DNA and RNA.\n\nVisible structure:\n- Four panels arranged horizontally.\n- Left two panels are grouped under the header “DNA”:\n  - “ID-test”\n  - “OOD-test”\n- Right two panels are grouped under the header “RNA”:\n  - “ID-test”\n  - “OOD-test”\n- Y-axis label: “Accuracy”, ranging approximately from 0.0 to 1.0.\n- X-axis categories in each panel:\n  - Base\n  - SFT\n  - RL\n  - CPT+SFT\n  - CPT+RL\n- Legend:\n  - Blue bars: Qwen3-1.7B\n  - Orange bars: Qwen3-4B\n\nBiological source objects:\n- DNA sequence tasks\n- RNA sequence tasks\n\nModel interfaces / transformations shown:\n- Comparison of baseline and training/adaptation strategies:\n  - Base\n  - supervised fine-tuning, SFT\n  - reinforcement learning, RL\n  - continued pretraining plus SFT, CPT+SFT\n  - continued pretraining plus RL, CPT+RL\n- Comparison across two model sizes: Qwen3-1.7B and Qwen3-4B.\n- Evaluation split comparison: in-distribution test and out-of-distribution test.\n\nVisible findings:\n- For DNA, RL and CPT+RL achieve the highest accuracies, especially on ID-test and OOD-test.\n- For RNA, Base accuracy is low, while SFT, RL, CPT+SFT, and CPT+RL substantially improve performance.\n- CPT+RL appears among the best-performing settings across DNA and RNA panels.\n- Qwen3-4B is generally similar to or slightly higher than Qwen3-1.7B in many conditions.\nWe next study whether continued pre-training changes how much downstream post-training can help. In the DNA and RNA settings, we first adapt the base backbones with continued pre-training on biological texts, yielding model families of the form Q1-D/R-C and Q4-D/R-C. We then evaluate these models under the strongest post-training configurations identified above, namely Q1-D-C-S 8 , 1 , Q4-D-C-S 4 , 1 , Q1-D-C-S 8 , 1 -R 16 , 1 , and Q4-D-C-S 4 , 1 -R 16 , 1 for DNA and Q1-R-C-S 4 , 1 , Q1-R-C-S 4 , 1 -R 8 , 1 , Q4-R-C-S 8 , 1 , and Q4-R-C-S 8 , 1 -R 8 , 1 for RNA. Figure 5 shows that CPT improves downstream performance at almost every stage we test, but that the size of the gain depends strongly on the stage. The gains are modest at the SFT stage in domain and markedly larger after RL, especially out-of-domain. Here, CPT lifts ID and OOD performance by visibly larger margins than SFT alone.",
./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/july_update_2026-07-06__rec_000050_23824e963093/july_update_2026_07_06_rec_000050_23824e963093_docling_json_20260812_141419/docling_graph/provenance.json:4593:      "text": "5 Discussion Appendices A Additional Details on Biological Reasoning Tasks, 1 = A Additional Details on Biological Reasoning Tasks. A Additional Details on Biological Reasoning Tasks, 2 = A Additional Details on Biological Reasoning Tasks. A Additional Details on Biological Reasoning Tasks, 3 = 19. , 1 = A.1. , 2 = Pathway Prediction Dataset and Construction . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .. , 3 = 19. , 1 = A.2. , 2 = Target Identification Dataset and Simplifications Relative to MEDEA . . . . . . . .. , 3 = 19. , 1 = A.3. , 2 = Protein Function Prediction Dataset and Simplifications Relative to BioReason-Pro. , 3 = 20. , 1 = A.4. , 2 = Example Prompts and Inputs for Each Task . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .. , 3 = 20. , 1 = Experimental Setup. , 2 = Experimental Setup. , 3 = 22. , 1 = B.1. , 2 = Base Models, Tokenization, and Input Representations . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .. , 3 = 22. , 1 = B.2. , 2 = Continued Pre-training (Mid-training) Setup . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .. , 3 = 22. , 1 = B.3. , 2 = Supervised Fine-Tuning Setup . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .. , 3 = 23. , 1 = B.4. , 2 = Reinforcement-Learning (GRPO) Setup . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .. , 3 = 23. , 1 = B.5. , 2 = LoRA Configurations and Trainable-Parameter Choices . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .. , 3 = 25. , 1 = B.6. , 2 = Hyperparameters, Context Windows, Sequence Lengths, and Optimization . . . . .. , 3 = 25. , 1 = B.7. , 2 = Compute Resources and Training Budget . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .. , 3 = 26. , 1 = Main-Results Tables. , 2 = Main-Results Tables. , 3 = 26. , 1 = C.1. , 2 = Results for Figure 2 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .