./data/living_catalog/taxonomy_rerun_preflight_2026-08-12/taxonomy/runs/discovery/shard_02/records/june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000248_de5b21014e76/june_update_2026_06_10_rec_000248_de5b21014e76_docling_json_20260812_141810/docling/document.md:412:Figure 5 shows a skewed distribution of the top 30 disease categories (out of 128), with normal (592,932) and COVID-19 (61,961) dominating. The high count for normal likely stems from extensive use of healthy control samples in research to establish baselines, while COVID-19's prominence reflects widespread data collection during the pandemic.
